  { id:'opqb-t18-716', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'The **first line of management** of an **acidic chemical injury** is:',
    options:['Wash with water immediately','Do not touch the eye, refer to a specialist','Give intravenous steroids then refer','Look for an alkaline antidote to wash with'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Copious irrigation** is very important to:\n\n1. Decrease the duration of contact with the chemical and,\n2. Bring the pH in the conjunctival sac back to normal as much as possible rapidly,\n3. The speed and efficacy of irrigation is the most important prognostic factor following chemical injury.\n\n**Technique:**\n\n1. 15-30 minutes or until the measured pH is neutral.\n2. Eversion of the upper eyelid: any retained particulate matter trapped in the conjunctival fornices is identified and removed by a sterile cotton swab.\n3. Debridement of necrotic areas of corneal epithelium at the slit lamp to promote re-epithelialization\n4. and remove associated residues of the chemical. (By an ophthalmologist)\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE WORD THAT MAKES THIS QUESTION HARD IS \"ACIDIC\", AND IT IS THERE TO BE IGNORED.** The stem specifies an acid, and three of the four options invite you to treat an acid differently from an alkali — refer it, steroid it, or neutralise it. **The chemistry does not change the first action.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 9 gives one instruction for chemical injury of any kind: irrigation **\"should be STARTED IMMEDIATELY by the health care provider and an ophthalmologist should be called\"**, with **saline 2 L for 15–30 min**.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Do not touch the eye, refer to a specialist** | ⚠️ **The most dangerous answer on the page.** `L25` slide 2 calls chemical injuries **\"the MOST URGENT ophthalmic emergency\"** — printed in red — and says they **\"require emergency management by the FIRST PERSON WHO SEES the affected patient\"**. Referring without irrigating spends the only minutes that matter. The referral happens too, but *after* the tap is running. |\n| **Give intravenous steroids then refer** | Steroids are real treatment and they come later. `L25` slide 12 puts **topical steroids** under *referral to ophthalmology emergencies*, alongside systemic ascorbic acid and doxycycline — the second phase, after the emergency measures of slides 9–11. Nothing systemic is given first, and nothing intravenous is given at all. |\n| **Look for an alkaline antidote to wash with** | ⚠️ **The trap for a chemist.** `L26) Trauma II` does print antidotes — **alkalis → boric acid 4 %, lime → EDTA, acids → Na bicarbonate 3 %, iodine → starch, aniline dyes → glycerine** — so an acid antidote exists on paper. But note where it sits: under **\"Emergency room\"**, several lines *after* **\"First aid treatment: copious irrigation (15-30 min)\"**. **Searching for an antidote is time spent not irrigating**, and a neutralisation reaction in the conjunctival sac is exothermic. Water now beats the right chemical in ten minutes. |\n\n**⚠️ The one point where acid and alkali genuinely differ is prognosis, not first aid.** `L25` slide 4 explains it: **alkalis \"cause mor [sic] damage as they tend to PENETRATE more deeply than acids and continue penetration for WEEKS\"**, while **acids \"COAGULATE SURFACE PROTEINS, forming a PROTECTIVE BARRIER against further penetration\"**. That is `opqb-t18-723`. It changes what you expect to happen next; it does not change what you do first.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cornea` “Cornea — infection, trauma and the red eye” — `cor-10` carries the corneal burn that is irrigated before it is examined.)*",
    objective:'Every chemical injury, acid or alkali, is irrigated immediately by whoever is present — before referral, before steroids and before any search for an antidote.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q716), key and box p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-717', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'All of the following tests are useful in evaluating a patient with a **retained iron intraocular foreign body EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Ultrasonography','CT scan','MRI','Indirect ophthalmoscopy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slide 52 prints the rule in capitals, as a prohibition rather than a preference: \"DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED.\"**\n\nIron is ferromagnetic. A scanner’s field will pull on it, and a fragment sitting quietly in the vitreous can be dragged through the retina, the lens or the optic nerve inside the bore of the magnet. *(The mechanism — torque and translation on a ferromagnetic fragment — is __not taken from the course material__; the prohibition itself is the slide’s.)*\n\n**The other three are the slide’s own investigation list:**\n\n| Test | `L25` slide 52 |\n|---|---|\n| **CT scan** | **\"Determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB\"** and **\"Show the shape and integrity of the traumatized globe.\"** The first-line scan. |\n| **Ultrasound** | **\"Ultrasound may be useful.\"** No magnet, and it works when blood or cataract blocks the view — but ⚠️ it is used with care on an eye that may be open, because it needs contact with the globe *(the caution is __not taken from the course material__)*. |\n| **Indirect ophthalmoscopy** | Looking in is looking. The deck’s four-step focused examination (`L25` slide 60) puts **ophthalmoscopy** third, for retinal detachment and vitreous haemorrhage. A fragment in the posterior segment can simply be seen. |\n\n⚠️ **Plain X-ray of the head is on the slide too**, first in the list, and it is not offered here — worth remembering, because it is cheap, available and detects metal.\n\n**⚠️ Read this beside `opqb-t18-739`, which tests the same rule from the other end** — *when is MRI contraindicated?* — and note that the two questions share not one option string. One enumerates modalities, the other enumerates foreign-body materials.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'MRI is prohibited when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected; CT, ultrasound, plain X-ray and indirect ophthalmoscopy are all part of the deck’s own workup.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q717), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-718', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **small intraocular iron foreign body**:',
    options:["Can be left if it doesn't harm the eye.",'Must be removed.','Necessitates enucleation.','Can only be removed if it produces cataract.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Iron is not inert, and that is the whole question.** `L25) Trauma I` slides 46–47 sort intraocular foreign bodies into exactly three classes, and the sorting is by what the material does to the eye over time:\n\n| Material | What follows |\n|---|---|\n| **Iron** | **SIDEROSIS** |\n| **Copper** | **CHALCOSIS** |\n| **Inert** | stone, glass, **pastic** [sic], wood, … |\n\nOnly the third class is called inert. An iron fragment slowly dissolves in the ocular fluids and iron is deposited in the epithelial tissues — lens epithelium, iris, ciliary body, retina — producing **siderosis bulbi**, which is `opqb-t18-738`. *(The tissue distribution and the timescale are __not taken from the course material__; the iron → siderosis pairing is the slide’s.)*\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Can be left if it doesn’t harm the eye** | ⚠️ **The size and the silence are the trap.** \"Small\" and \"not harming the eye today\" describe every siderotic fragment on the day it arrives. The damage is cumulative and it is not reversed by removing the fragment late. |\n| **Necessitates enucleation** | Enucleation removes the eye. It is a treatment for a blind painful eye or a tumour (`opqb-t3-127`), not for a fragment that can be taken out through a vitrectomy. |\n| **Can only be removed if it produces cataract** | Same error as A, with a specific endpoint. A siderotic cataract is one late manifestation; waiting for it means waiting for retinal damage too. |\n\n**⚠️ And the presence of an IOFB is itself a diagnosis, not just a finding.** `L25` slide 52 opens: **\"The presence of an intraocular foreign body (IOFB) suggests globe PENETRATION.\"** A fragment inside the eye means the eye is open, which brings in the whole open-globe protocol of `opqb-t18-736` and `opqb-t18-737`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An iron intraocular foreign body is never inert — it must be removed, because retained iron produces siderosis bulbi whatever its size.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q718), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-719', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**Symblepharon** may be caused by:',
    options:['Chemical burn.','Spring catarrh.','Phlycten.','Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A symblepharon is an adhesion between the palpebral and the bulbar conjunctiva, so it needs raw surfaces on both sides of the fornix at the same time.** A chemical burn is the injury that produces exactly that, and both trauma decks say so.\n\n`L25) Trauma I` slide 6 lists the late consequences of a chemical injury: **\"Later, dry eye, SYMBLEPHARON, cicatricial entropion, corneal scarring and vascularization can develop.\"** `L26) Trauma II` reaches it by a different route — its **late or chronic phase (> 7 days)** is headed **\"Cicatrization\"**, and under conjunctiva prints **\"scarring → dry eye, SYMBLEPHARON, entropion.\"**\n\n**⚠️ The deck also gives the prophylaxis, which is the memorable part.** `L25` slide 12 lists **\"Prevention of SYMBLEPHARON formation by lysis of newly forming adhesions by a sterile GLASS ROD\"** — a rod swept round the fornix to break the adhesions while they are still soft. `L26` puts **\"symblepharon management\"** first among the surgical treatments of complications.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | What it actually does to the conjunctiva |\n|---|---|\n| **Spring catarrh** | Vernal keratoconjunctivitis. It gives **giant cobblestone papillae** on the upper tarsus and limbal Horner–Trantas dots (`cnj-6`), and it can scar the cornea through a shield ulcer — but the papillary reaction is a hypertrophy of the epithelium and stroma, not a full-thickness ulceration facing another one. ⚠️ It is on this list because it is the other chronic conjunctival disease students remember, not because it does this. |\n| **Phlycten** | A small nodular hypersensitivity lesion at the limbus or on the bulbar conjunctiva (`L6) Conjunctiva`). It is tiny, self-limiting and bulbar — there is no opposing palpebral lesion for it to stick to. |\n| **Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis** | Adenoviral. It can produce a **conjunctival MEMBRANE or pseudomembrane** in a severe case (`cnj-3`) and, rarely, that can scar — but the module teaches EKC as a self-limiting follicular conjunctivitis with subepithelial corneal infiltrates, and names no cicatricial sequel. |\n\n**⚠️ The one non-trauma cause the module does teach is not on this list:** `L6) Conjunctiva` and `ophthalmology.pdf` chapter 6 give **cicatricial trachoma** the same fornix-obliterating scarring, and the WHO grade **TS** is exactly that fibrosis. If trachoma had been offered here it would also have been correct.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-conj` “Conjunctiva” — `cnj-11` carries the cicatricial trachoma that scars the same fornix, and `cnj-5`–`cnj-8` carry the three distractors.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Symblepharon is the cicatricial sequel of a chemical burn — the injury that leaves raw palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva facing each other — and it is prevented by sweeping the fornix with a glass rod.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q719), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-720', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Concerning **chemical injuries of the eye**:',
    options:['Acids cause more serious damage than alkalis.','The white eye is a sign of mild affection.','EDTA eye drops & penicillamine helps collagen synthesis.','A soft bandage contact lens is used in presence of secondary mucopurulent conjunctivitis.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A DISAGREEMENT WITH THE PRINTED KEY, RECORDED AND NOT DISPUTED. The key is C and it is keyed C here. `L26) Trauma II` prints the mechanism of those two drugs, and it is not the one the option states.**\n\n`L26` sets out the medical treatment of a chemical burn in three tiers, and both halves of the confusion are on the same slide:\n\n| Tier | Drug | `L26`’s own purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topical **and** systemic | **Ascorbic acid** | **\"to enhance COLLAGEN PRODUCTION\"** |\n| Topical **and** systemic | **Tetracyclines** | **\"to inhibit COLLAGENASE and neutrophil activity\"** |\n| **Systemic** | **Ca chelators: EDTA, PENICILLAMINE** | **\"to inhibit COLLAGENASE\"** |\n\nSo the deck gives EDTA and penicillamine an **anti-collagenase** role, and gives **collagen synthesis to ascorbic acid**. Option C attaches the right pair of drugs to the wrong half of the slide. *(`L25) Trauma I` slide 12 agrees on the principle from the other side, listing **\"Systemic ascorbic acid\"** and **\"Systemic DOXYCYCLINE as collagenase inhibitors\"** — a tetracycline, again as the inhibitor.)*\n\n**⚠️ AND YET C IS THE ONLY OPTION THAT IS NOT FLATLY FALSE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS KEYED.** Each of the other three is contradicted outright by the decks:\n\n| Option | The contradiction |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Acids cause more serious damage than alkalis** | Reversed. `L25` slide 4: alkalis **\"cause mor [sic] damage as they tend to PENETRATE more deeply than acids and continue penetration for WEEKS\"**, while acids **\"COAGULATE SURFACE PROTEINS, forming a PROTECTIVE BARRIER\"**. `L25` slide 2 adds that alkali burns are **twice as common**. |\n| **B. The white eye is a sign of mild affection** | ⚠️ **The single most dangerous misreading in the whole chemical-injury section, and the reason it is offered.** A quiet white eye after a chemical splash is not a mild eye — it is a **limbally ischaemic** one. `L25` slide 5: **\"Occlusion of the limbal vessels (LIMBAL ISCHAEMIA) and loss of LIMBAL STEM CELLS may lead to persistent corneal epithelial defects with sterile corneal ulceration and perforation.\"** The redness you would expect is absent *because the vessels have been closed*, and limbal ischaemia is the axis the entire grading scale is built on (`opqb-t18-725`). Blanching is grade 4 territory, not grade 1. |\n| **D. A soft bandage contact lens is used in presence of secondary mucopurulent conjunctivitis** | `L26` does list **\"Bandage soft contact lenses\"** among its medical measures — so the lens is real. The error is the *indication*. A bandage lens is worn to protect a persistent epithelial defect; putting one on an eye with **active mucopurulent discharge** traps organisms against a de-epithelialised cornea. *(The infection risk is __not taken from the course material__; `L26` prints the lens without any contraindication.)* |\n\n**How to hold this in an exam.** The bank is testing recognition of the *drug list*, not of the enzymology — it wants you to know that EDTA and penicillamine belong to chemical-burn treatment at all. **The claim it attaches to them is wrong, and the correction is worth more than the mark**: ascorbate builds collagen, tetracyclines and chelators stop it being broken down. Both arms of that are aimed at the same enemy, the sterile melt of `L25` slide 5.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Alkalis penetrate deeper than acids, a white eye after a chemical splash means limbal ischaemia rather than a mild injury, and EDTA and penicillamine are collagenase inhibitors — the deck gives collagen production to ascorbic acid.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q720), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-721', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-optic',
    stem:'Concerning **traumatic optic atrophy**:',
    options:['Loss of vision takes 2-3 months to appear.','Pallor of the optic disc is immediate.','It is due to damage of the central retinal artery.','May be due to trivial blunt injury to the brow.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THE MODULE TEACHES THIS NOWHERE, AND THE ABSENCE WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED.** `traumatic optic` returns **exactly four hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters, and not one of them is teaching**: `L21) Optic Nerve` item 8 of its own not-covered list (**\"LEBER’S HEREDITARY OPTIC NEUROPATHY, OPTIC NERVE GLIOMA, OPTIC NERVE SHEATH MENINGIOMA and TRAUMATIC OPTIC NEUROPATHY are absent entirely — no slide, no caption, no line\"**), `L25) Trauma I`’s not-covered list, `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.58** as the deferral **\"or traumatic optic neuropathy. (Refer to trauma chapter)\"**, and chapter 14’s not-covered item 20. **The referral has no destination — the trauma chapter never picks it up.** Everything below is __not taken from the course material__.\n\n**Why D is right: the injury is out of all proportion to the damage.** Most traumatic optic neuropathy is **indirect** — no penetrating wound, no fracture through the canal, often no visible mark. A blow to the **brow or the lateral orbital rim** transmits force through the frontal bone to the **optic canal**, where the nerve is tethered in its dural sheath and cannot move. The shearing tears the tiny pial vessels that supply the intracanalicular segment. A fall from a bicycle onto the eyebrow is a classic mechanism, and the patient may have nothing to show but an eye that cannot see.\n\n**The finding is an RAPD in an eye with a normal fundus.** That mismatch — profound loss, a brisk relative afferent pupillary defect, and a disc that looks entirely ordinary — is the diagnosis.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and the timing is the point:**\n\n| Option | What is actually true |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Loss of vision takes 2-3 months to appear** | ⚠️ **Exactly inverted, and this is the pairing the question is built on.** The **vision is lost at the moment of impact**; it is the **disc pallor** that takes 4–8 weeks, because the axons are damaged at the canal and the atrophy has to travel back to the disc. |\n| **B. Pallor of the optic disc is immediate** | The other half of the same inversion. On day one the disc is normal — which is why a patient with a normal-looking fundus and no vision is missed unless the pupils are examined. |\n| **C. It is due to damage of the central retinal artery** | A central retinal artery occlusion produces a **white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot** — `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` case 1 and `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders`. In traumatic optic neuropathy the **retina is normal**, because the injury is behind the globe and the retinal circulation enters the nerve in front of it. |\n\n**⚠️ Why the module’s own optic-atrophy teaching still applies.** `onv-13` gives four types of optic atrophy — primary, secondary, consecutive and glaucomatous — as four captions on `L21` slide 14. Traumatic optic atrophy is a **primary** (descending) atrophy: the disc goes chalk-white with sharp margins and no preceding swelling, because the lesion is retrobulbar. The module names the type without ever naming this cause of it.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — the blunt injury behind it; `trm-7` records this entity by name among the five things neither trauma deck prints.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Traumatic optic neuropathy follows a trivial blow to the brow, takes the vision instantly and leaves an RAPD with a normal fundus; the disc pallor is what waits weeks, and the retinal circulation is untouched.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q721), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-722', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:'The **most common bone affected in blow-out fracture** is:',
    options:['Lacrimal','Ethmoidal','Maxillary','Frontal'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Every source in the module says the FLOOR, and the floor is the maxilla.**\n\n`L25) Trauma I` slide 19 titles the entity **\"Blow-out fracture of the ORBITAL FLOOR\"** and slide 23 asks CT for **\"fracture of the orbital floor ± other orbital wall\"**. `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.58** is more explicit: **\"The fracture most frequently involves THE FLOOR OF THE ORBIT along the thin bone covering the infraorbital canal. Sometimes, the medial orbital wall may also be fractured.\"** The book’s own Fig (3-3) on **p.59** is captioned **\"CT scan showing Blow out fracture of orbital floor\"** and shows soft tissue **herniating into the MAXILLARY SINUS** — which is the anatomical statement this question wants, made in a figure caption: the orbital floor is the roof of the maxillary sinus, so the bone that breaks is the **orbital plate of the maxilla**. *(The words \"orbital plate of the maxilla\" are printed by no cached source; the floor/maxillary-sinus relationship is the book’s and `orb-14`’s. __Not taken from the course material__ as a named bone.)*\n\n**Why the mechanism produces this bone and no other.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58: the fracture is **\"caused by a sudden increase in the orbital pressure from an impacting object that is GREATER IN DIAMETER THAN THE ORBITAL APERTURE (fist, tennis ball)\"**. The rim is thick and takes the blow intact; the pressure is transmitted inward and the thinnest bone gives way. The floor and the medial wall are the two thin ones, and the floor is thinner still where the **infraorbital groove and canal** run through it.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Bone | Where it sits, and why it survives |\n|---|---|\n| **Lacrimal** | The smallest bone in the orbit, in the **anterior medial wall** at the lacrimal fossa. Thin, but shielded within the medial wall complex and outside the line of transmitted pressure. |\n| **Ethmoidal** | ⚠️ **The genuine near-miss, and the module keys it elsewhere.** The **lamina papyracea** of the ethmoid is the medial wall — literally \"paper-thin\" — and the book says it is fractured *\"sometimes\"*, second to the floor. **And `opqb-t5-189`, in this same bank, keys ETHMOIDAL for \"surgical emphysema of the lid\"**, because the air that tracks into the lids comes from the ethmoid air cells. **Both keys are correct: the bone that most often breaks is the maxilla, and the bone that most often lets air through is the ethmoid.** Hold the two questions together. |\n| **Frontal** | Forms the **roof**. `L25`’s not-covered list records orbital roof fractures as untaught, and the roof is thick bone against the frontal sinus and the anterior cranial fossa — a roof fracture is a neurosurgical injury, not a blow-out. |\n\n**⚠️ Distinguish this from `opmcq-c3-27`, which asks the same fact on a different axis** — *which orbital WALL* — with options Roof / Medial / Floor / Lateral. The two questions share no option string, and knowing \"the floor\" does not automatically give you \"the maxilla\".\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — the blunt injury itself; `orb-14` carries the full blow-out account and `trm-7` points at it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A blow-out fracture breaks the orbital floor, and the orbital floor is the maxilla; the ethmoid lamina papyracea is the second wall to go and the bone that lets air into the lids.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q722), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-723', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Which of the following statements about **alkali burns** is **TRUE**?',
    options:['Acids penetrate deeper than alkalis','Alkalis cause coagulation necrosis','Alkalis penetrate deeper and continue for weeks','Acids cause worse limbal ischemia'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slide 4 is one sentence long and it answers this question and its three distractors together:**\n\n> **ALKALIS** cause **mor** [sic] **damage as they tend to PENETRATE MORE DEEPLY than acids and CONTINUE PENETRATION FOR WEEKS.**\n> On the other hand, **ACIDS COAGULATE SURFACE PROTEINS, forming a PROTECTIVE BARRIER against further penetration.**\n\n`L26) Trauma II` gives the chemistry behind it, naming the agents on both sides:\n\n| | Agents | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Alkalis** | **NaOH, KOH, Ca(OH)₂, NH₄** | **\"Combine with cell membrane lipid and stromal mucopolysaccharides → deep penetration\"** |\n| **Acids** | **H₂SO₄, HCl, HNO₃, hypochlorite** | **\"Coagulate tissue proteins → less penetration\"** |\n\n**⚠️ THE WORD THAT MATTERS IS \"CONTINUE\".** An acid burn is essentially over when it is washed off — the coagulum it made in the first seconds is the barrier that stops it. An alkali is still advancing days later, which is why `L26`’s staging runs to a **late or chronic phase beyond 7 days** and why the follow-up is measured in weeks. And `L25` slide 2 adds the epidemiology: **alkali burns are TWICE AS COMMON as acid burns, since alkalis are more widely used both at home and in industry** — drain cleaner, oven cleaner, plaster, cement, ammonia.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Acids penetrate deeper than alkalis** | The statement reversed. |\n| **B. Alkalis cause coagulation necrosis** | ⚠️ **The best-constructed distractor on the page**, because coagulation necrosis is a real thing that happens in a real chemical burn — it is just the **acid’s** mechanism. The alkali equivalent is **liquefactive** necrosis, which is exactly why it does not stop *(the word \"liquefactive\" is printed by neither trauma deck — __not taken from the course material__; the \"coagulate surface proteins\" half is `L25`’s)*. |\n| **D. Acids cause worse limbal ischemia** | Limbal ischaemia is the axis of the grading scale (`opqb-t18-725`) and it is worse in the injury that goes deeper — the alkali. `L25` slide 5 attributes it to **\"occlusion of the limbal vessels\"** with **loss of limbal stem cells**, which is a consequence of depth, not of pH direction. |\n\n**⚠️ None of this changes the first action.** `opqb-t18-716` is the pair to this one: alkalis are worse, and both are irrigated immediately and identically.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Alkalis penetrate deeply and go on penetrating for weeks, because they saponify membrane lipid; acids coagulate surface protein and build their own barrier, which is why an acid burn largely stops when it is washed off.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.115 (Q723), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-724', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **32-year-old factory worker** presents immediately after a splash of a cleaning agent into his eye. Eye is **extremely painful and vision is blurred**. **pH paper shows alkaline pH**. **After irrigation**, which treatment is appropriate?',
    options:['Topical tetracycline','Oral steroids only','No further management needed','Immediate keratoplasty'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The stem has already done the emergency step, so this question is about the phase after it — and `L26) Trauma II` prints tetracyclines in that phase by name.**\n\n`L26`’s medical treatment of a chemical burn:\n\n| Route | Agent | `L26`’s stated purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topical | **Cycloplegic** · **antibiotic** · **steroid** · **citric acid** · lubricants | steroid **\"to decrease inflammation\"**, citrate **\"to inhibit neutrophil activity\"** |\n| Topical **and** systemic | **Ascorbic acid** | **\"to enhance collagen production\"** |\n| Topical **and** systemic | **TETRACYCLINES** | **\"to inhibit COLLAGENASE and neutrophil activity\"** |\n| Systemic | Ca chelators — EDTA, penicillamine | **\"to inhibit collagenase\"** |\n\n`L25) Trauma I` slide 12 names the same drug class in the same role — **\"Systemic DOXYCYCLINE as collagenase inhibitors\"** — so both decks put a tetracycline in the burnt eye, and both put it there for its **anti-collagenase** effect rather than its antibacterial one.\n\n**⚠️ Why the enemy is collagenase.** `L25` slide 5 describes what kills these eyes: loss of limbal stem cells → **persistent corneal epithelial defect** → **sterile corneal ulceration and PERFORATION**. That melt is enzymatic. The tetracycline is not treating an infection; it is trying to stop the cornea digesting itself.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Oral steroids only** | Steroids are used — `L26` prints **topical** steroid drops and `L25` slide 12 puts topical steroids under referral. ⚠️ **The killer word is \"only\".** A steroid alone suppresses inflammation while doing nothing about collagenase, and steroids are themselves suspected of impairing stromal healing if continued past the first week *(the healing caution is __not taken from the course material__)*. Neither deck offers an oral steroid at all. |\n| **No further management needed** | ⚠️ **The mistake that follows a good irrigation.** Both decks build multi-week regimens *after* the pH is neutral, and `L26`’s **late or chronic phase (> 7 days)** — scarring, dry eye, symblepharon, entropion, a second IOP rise from **scarring of the episcleral venous plexus** — all happens after the patient has been irrigated and feels better. |\n| **Immediate keratoplasty** | Keratoplasty is on `L25` slide 13, under **surgical intervention**, alongside limbal stem cell transplantation, amniotic membrane grafting and keratoprosthesis — and it is **\"for OPAQUE CORNEAS\"**, i.e. for a scarred eye months later. Grafting onto an acutely burnt, inflamed, stem-cell-deficient bed fails. |\n\n**⚠️ Note what the stem does not give you: a grade.** It says painful and blurred but never describes the cornea or the limbus, so this is deliberately a question about the general regimen and not about `opqb-t18-725`’s grading.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'After irrigation an alkali burn gets a medical regimen — cycloplegic, antibiotic, steroid, ascorbate and a tetracycline — and the tetracycline is there to inhibit collagenase and prevent the sterile corneal melt.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q724), key p.119' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-725', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **55-year-old male patient** presents after a **cement splash**. Exam: **hazy cornea but iris details are visible; limbal ischemia <30%**. What **grade** is this injury?',
    options:['Grade 1','Grade 2','Grade 3','Grade 4'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Grading depends on corneal clarity and severity of limbal ischemia.**\n\n**Grade 1**\n- Clear cornea (epithelial damage only)\n- No limbal ischemia,\n- Excellent Prognosis\n\n**Grade 2**\n- **Hazy cornea but with visible iris detail and less than 30% limbal ischemia.**\n- **good prognosis**\n\n**Grade 3**\n- Total loss of corneal epithelium\n- Stromal haze obscuring iris detail\n- 1/3 to 1/2 limbal ischemia\n- Guarded prognosis\n\n**Grade 4**\n- Opaque cornea\n- More than 50% limbal ischemia.\n- Poor prognosis\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The stem is the grade-2 line copied out.** *Hazy cornea* + *iris details visible* + *limbal ischaemia under 30 %* — three criteria, three matches.\n\n**⚠️ THE TWO AXES ARE READ TOGETHER, AND THE LIMBUS IS THE ONE THAT DECIDES THE PROGNOSIS.** The cornea tells you what has happened; the limbus tells you what will happen. `L25) Trauma I` slide 5 explains why: the limbus carries the **stem cells** that resurface the cornea, and **\"occlusion of the limbal vessels (LIMBAL ISCHAEMIA) and loss of LIMBAL STEM CELLS may lead to persistent corneal epithelial defects with sterile corneal ulceration and perforation.\"** A hazy cornea over a living limbus re-epithelialises. A clear-looking cornea over a dead limbus does not.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE DECKS PRINT THE SAME SCALE IN FRACTIONS, NOT PERCENTAGES.** `L25` slide 7 and `L26) Trauma II` both grade the limbal ischaemia as **nil · < 1/3 · 1/3–1/2 · > 1/2**, where the printed box here says **no · <30 % · 1/3 to 1/2 · >50 %**. ⚠️ `<30 %` is not exactly `<1/3` (33 %) and `>50 %` is not exactly `>1/2` in the sense the decks use it, but the boundaries are the same boundaries and no case falls between them. The stem gives **<30 %**, which is inside grade 2 on either wording.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE DECKS DISAGREE ABOUT ONE WORD OF PROGNOSIS.** `L25` slide 7 gives grade 1 **\"good\"**; `L26` gives grade 1 **\"excellent\"** and grade 4 **\"very poor\"**. The printed box here follows `L26` — excellent / good / guarded / poor. The four grades and the four ischaemia fractions are identical in all three sources; only the adjectives move. **Recorded, not resolved.**\n\n**⚠️ Cement is an alkali**, and a particularly bad one: it is **lime**, calcium hydroxide, so it both burns and leaves gritty particles in the fornices that go on releasing alkali until they are swabbed out. `L25` slide 10 exists for exactly this — **eversion of the upper eyelid** and removal of retained particulate matter with a sterile cotton swab — and `L26` names the specific antidote, **lime → EDTA**.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cornea` “Cornea — infection, trauma and the red eye” — `cor-10` carries the burn that is irrigated before it is examined.)*",
    objective:'A hazy cornea through which iris detail is still visible, with under a third of the limbus ischaemic, is a grade 2 chemical injury with a good prognosis — and it is the limbal ischaemia, not the corneal haze, that predicts the outcome.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q725), key and box p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-726', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'For the **55-year-old man after a cement splash**, whose cornea is **hazy with iris details still visible** and whose **limbal ischaemia is under 30 %** — is **hospital admission required**?',
    options:['Yes, according to age','Yes, depend on grade','No, outpatient only','Only for acids'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints this stem as **\"In the previous Q…\"**, and the previous Q is `opqb-t18-725`. The clinical findings are restated; the **grade** is deliberately **not** restated, because the grade is what this question turns on.)*\n\n**The principle is the one `L25) Trauma I` states about hyphaema and applies throughout the trauma decks: admission is a decision, not a reflex.** Slide 28 prints it in that form — **\"Hospitalization: NOT EVERY CASE\"** — followed by the list of who does get admitted.\n\nFor a chemical injury the deciding variable is the **grade**, because the grade is the prognosis. The scale (`opqb-t18-725`) runs **excellent → good → guarded → poor**, and it is built out of corneal clarity and limbal ischaemia:\n\n| Grade | What it means for admission |\n|---|---|\n| **1–2** | Cornea recoverable, limbus largely alive. `L25` slide 11’s topical regimen — cycloplegia, antibiotic, patching — can be given and reviewed as an outpatient. |\n| **3–4** | **Total epithelial loss or an opaque cornea with a third to more than half the limbus ischaemic.** These are the eyes that go on to **sterile ulceration and perforation** (`L25` slide 5) and that need the referral measures of slide 12 — topical steroid, systemic ascorbate, systemic doxycycline, IOP control, and **daily glass-rod lysis of forming symblepharon adhesions**. That is inpatient work. |\n\n*(The grade-by-grade admission threshold is __not taken from the course material__ — neither trauma deck prints an admission rule for chemical injury. What the decks supply is the grading scale, its prognostic meaning, and the principle from slide 28 that hospitalisation is selective.)*\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Yes, according to age** | ⚠️ **Age IS an admission criterion in this module — for the wrong condition.** `L25` slide 28 admits **children** with hyphaema, because they cannot be relied on to rest and cannot report a re-bleed. A 55-year-old adult with a chemical burn is not admitted for being 55. |\n| **No, outpatient only** | True for this patient and false as a rule. The option is an absolute, and the correct answer is a conditional. |\n| **Only for acids** | Backwards, and it is the topic’s recurring trap. `L25` slide 4: **alkalis** penetrate more deeply and **continue for weeks**; slide 2: alkali burns are **twice as common**. If either agent justified an automatic admission it would be the alkali — and this patient’s cement is an alkali. |\n\n**⚠️ The clinical reason the grade decides.** A grade 1–2 eye needs drops and a review. A grade 3–4 eye needs someone to look at it every day: the limbal stem cells are gone, the epithelium is not closing, and the two things that will blind it — **a melt** and a **symblepharon sealing the fornix shut** — both develop over days and both are treatable only if they are caught while they are forming.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Admission after a chemical injury is decided by the grade, because the grade is the prognosis — not by the patient’s age and not by whether the agent was an acid.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q726), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-727', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:'A man is **punched in the eye**. Within minutes: **proptosis, severe pain, decreased vision, tight lids, RAPD**. What is the **NEXT immediate management**?',
    options:['CT Orbit','MRI orbit','Lateral canthotomy + cantholysis','Cold compress'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**This is a retrobulbar haemorrhage behaving as an ORBITAL COMPARTMENT SYNDROME, and the treatment is a bedside cut, not a scan.**\n\n**⚠️ MEASURED: NO CACHED DECK DESCRIBES THIS ENTITY.** `canthotomy`, `cantholysis` and `compartment` each return **exactly one hit across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters, and all three hits are inside `L25) Trauma I`’s own NOT-COVERED list** — which records the gap in these words: *\"RETROBULBAR HAEMORRHAGE / ORBITAL COMPARTMENT SYNDROME as a named entity — its signs, its IOP rise, and lateral canthotomy/cantholysis. The deck names ecchymosis, surgical emphysema and orbital floor fracture but never the retrobulbar bleed.\"* What the module does supply is the entity’s **name and its urgency**: `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.58** lists **\"Orbital/ Retrobulbar haemorrhage: (refer to ocular trauma chapter) — acute onset · history of trauma (or surgery) · OPHTHALMIC EMERGENCY DUE TO MARKEDLY RAISED IOP WITH RISK OF CRAO\"**, and `L10) orbit` slide 6 names **\"retrobulbar hge\"** as a cause of proptosis by pushing the globe forward. The management below is __not taken from the course material__.\n\n**Why the orbit cannot wait.** The orbit is a bony cone closed in front by the septum and the globe. Blood behind the eye has nowhere to go, so pressure rises until it exceeds the perfusion pressure of the **central retinal artery** and the pial vessels of the optic nerve. **The retina and the nerve then infarct, and the window is roughly 60–90 minutes.** Every sign in the stem is that pressure being transmitted somewhere:\n\n| Sign in the stem | What it reports |\n|---|---|\n| **Proptosis** | the globe is the only mobile wall of the cone |\n| **Severe pain** | orbital tissue under stretch |\n| **TIGHT LIDS** | the septum under tension — the most specific sign, and the reason the lids will not evert |\n| **Decreased vision + RAPD** | **the emergency itself** — optic nerve and retinal perfusion already failing |\n\n**Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis is the decompression.** The lateral canthal tendon is divided and its inferior crus released, which lets the lower lid and the globe move forward and drops the orbital pressure at once. It takes a minute, needs local anaesthetic and scissors, and is done **before** imaging.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **CT Orbit** | ⚠️ **The most attractive wrong answer, because it is what you would order for almost any other orbital trauma** — `opmcq-c3-11` keys CT for assessing an orbital floor fracture, and `L25` slide 23 asks for a coronal CT. **But this diagnosis is made by looking at the patient**, and a CT costs the twenty minutes the nerve does not have. Image afterwards. |\n| **MRI orbit** | Slower still, and **contraindicated outright if a metallic foreign body is possible** (`L25` slide 52, and `opqb-t18-717` and `opqb-t18-739` in this topic). |\n| **Cold compress** | The treatment for the **ecchymosis** — the black eye of `L25` slide 17 — which is the harmless thing this presentation is mistaken for. ⚠️ **A black eye does not take the vision and does not give an RAPD.** Those two findings are what separate a bruise from a blinding emergency. |\n\n**⚠️ AND THE PUPIL GOES BIG, NEVER SMALL.** `orb-13` makes the point against `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway`, whose causes of miosis are parasympathomimetics, Horner’s, pontine haemorrhage, acute iridocyclitis and trauma — orbital pressure is not among them. **A small pupil in an injured eye means traumatic iritis or a ruptured globe, not this.** That is what `opqb-t3-121` keys.\n\n**⚠️ Read with `opqb-t3-132`, which prints the same vignette and asks which SIGN is expected (ophthalmoplegia).** Same patient, opposite half of the question: that one asks what you will find, this one asks what you will do. Neither answers the other.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — the blunt injury behind it; `L25`’s own not-covered list records this entity as the gap `orb-13` fills.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Proptosis with tight lids, falling vision and an RAPD minutes after a blow is an orbital compartment syndrome from retrobulbar haemorrhage, and it is decompressed by immediate lateral canthotomy and cantholysis — before any imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q727), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-728', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:'In the man **punched in the eye** who developed, within minutes, **proptosis, severe pain, decreased vision, tight lids and an RAPD** — what is the **pathophysiology**?',
    options:['Neuropathy','Optic nerve compression & raised orbital pressure','Corneal perforation','Iris root avulsion'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(Back-reference repaired, and this is **the worst-damaged stem in the topic**: the bank prints it as the four bare words **\"What is the pathophysiology?\"** — no \"previous Q\", no patient, no condition, no finding of any kind. It is the first question in the RIGHT column of book p.116 and its antecedent, `opqb-t18-727`, is the last question in the LEFT column of the same page, so the only thing linking them on paper is the column break. The link was confirmed from the **option list**, not from position. The signs are restated here; `opqb-t18-727`’s answer — the canthotomy — is deliberately **not**.)*\n\n**Two mechanisms, one sentence, and both halves are in the option.**\n\n1. **Raised orbital pressure.** Blood accumulating behind the globe in a closed bony cone. The orbit has one compliant wall — the septum in front — and once that is tense the pressure has nowhere to go. It is what produces every sign in the stem: **proptosis** (the globe pushed forward), **tight lids** (the septum stretched), **pain**, and a **raised IOP** transmitted into the eye. `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.58** names the consequence explicitly — **\"ophthalmic emergency due to markedly raised IOP with risk of CRAO\"** — and `L10) orbit` slide 6 gives the mechanism as **\"the globe being pushed forward (retrobulbar hge, tumor etc)\"**.\n\n2. **Optic nerve compression.** The nerve is squeezed within the orbital apex and its pial blood supply fails. **That is what the RAPD in the stem is reporting**, and it is why the vision falls within minutes rather than hours. *(The apical compression and the pial supply are __not taken from the course material__; the raised-IOP-with-CRAO-risk half is the book’s.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE OPTION LIST STRADDLES TWO QUESTIONS, WHICH IS HOW THE ANTECEDENT WAS PROVED.** Option D, *iris root avulsion*, is the mechanism of the **hyphaema** in `opqb-t18-729`, one number later — the bank has printed a distractor drawn from the *next* vignette. That is also why this stem cannot be read as belonging to Q729: only option B fits a patient with proptosis and tight lids.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Neuropathy** | ⚠️ **Not wrong so much as empty.** The optic nerve is indeed failing, so \"neuropathy\" is a description of the outcome — but it names no mechanism, and the question asks for one. Compare option B, which says *why* the nerve is in trouble. ⚠️ It is also the word that would fit **traumatic optic neuropathy** (`opqb-t18-721`), a completely different injury in which the orbit is soft, the lids are normal and there is no proptosis at all. |\n| **Corneal perforation** | A corneal perforation gives a **shallow or flat anterior chamber, a peaked pupil and a positive Seidel test** (`L25` slides 49–51). It **decompresses** the eye rather than pressurising the orbit, and it produces no proptosis. |\n| **Iris root avulsion** | Iridodialysis — the iris root torn from the ciliary body, `L25` slide 29. It is the **source of the bleeding in a hyphaema** (`opqb-t18-729`’s printed box: *\"The source of bleeding is the iris root\"*) and it belongs to the anterior chamber, not the orbit. |\n\n**⚠️ The distinction worth carrying out of this pair:** blood **in front of the iris** is a hyphaema, watched and rested; blood **behind the globe** is a compartment syndrome, cut open within the hour. Both follow a punch, and this topic tests them two numbers apart.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — the blunt injury behind it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retrobulbar haemorrhage blinds by two linked mechanisms — pressure rising inside a closed bony orbit and compression of the optic nerve at the apex — which is what the proptosis, tight lids and RAPD are each reporting.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q728), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-729', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **17-year-old boy hit with a football** develops a **fluid blood level in the anterior chamber**. **IOP is elevated.** What structure is the **source of bleeding**?',
    options:['Ciliary body','Lens capsule','Iris root','Choroid'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"Hemorrhage in the anterior chamber.\n\n- The source of bleeding is **the iris root.**\n- The blood settles inferiorly with a resultant 'fluid level' except when the hyphemia is total.\n- **\"Eight-ball hyphaema\"**\n\n1. It is a total hyphemia with deoxygenation of the blood in the AC making it dark in color resembling the number 8 black ball of billiard.\n2. Its presence carries a higher risk of complications\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank’s own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim — including its own spelling \"hyphemia\", printed twice, beside the correct \"hyphaema\" inside the quoted phrase. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Why the iris root and not the ciliary body, which is the near-miss.** A blunt blow drives the equator of the globe outward, and the anterior chamber angle takes the shear. The vessels that tear are the ones running at the **iris root**, where the iris meets the ciliary body — the same tear that, if it separates the root completely, is the **iridodialysis** of `L25) Trauma I` slide 29 and of `opqb-t18-751`. ⚠️ **The ciliary body is anatomically adjacent and is where an angle recession sits**, which is why option A is offered; the bank has keyed the structure its own box names.\n\n**⚠️ THE FLUID LEVEL IS DIAGNOSTIC AND IT IS GRAVITY.** Free blood in the aqueous settles inferiorly and gives a flat meniscus — which is what separates a hyphaema from a diffuse anterior chamber haze. The exception the box names is a **total** hyphaema: with the chamber full there is no interface to see.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE EIGHT-BALL IS A CIRCULATION FAILURE, NOT AN OLD BLEED.** `L25` slide 26 gives the mechanism in one line: **\"Due to decreased oxygen the initially red blood turns very dark (impaired aqueous circulation).\"** A total hyphaema blocks the trabecular outflow, aqueous stops turning over, the blood deoxygenates, and it goes from red to near-black. **That colour is a warning, not a curiosity** — it means the chamber is full, the pressure is up, and the eye is at the top of `L25` slide 27’s complication list.\n\n**Grading, from `L25` slide 25:**\n\n| Grade | Anterior chamber filled |\n|---|---|\n| **I** | **< 1/3** |\n| **II** | **1/3 – 1/2** |\n| **III** | **> 1/2** |\n| **IV** | **TOTAL** |\n\n**Why the other two:**\n\n- **Lens capsule** is avascular. It can be ruptured by the same blow — that is the rosette cataract of `opqb-t18-744` — but it cannot bleed.\n- **Choroid** bleeding goes **backwards**, into the suprachoroidal space or the vitreous, not forwards into the anterior chamber. `L25` slide 39’s choroidal rupture is a posterior lesion (`opqb-t10-417`).\n\n**⚠️ The elevated IOP in the stem is the reason this boy is not simply sent home** — see `opqb-t18-730` and `opqb-t18-753`.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-uvea` “Uvea” — the iris root and the ciliary body are `uv-1` and `uv-2`.)*",
    objective:'Blunt trauma tears the vessels at the iris root, and the blood settles as a fluid level unless the hyphaema is total — in which case failed aqueous circulation deoxygenates it into an eight-ball hyphaema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q729), key and box p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-730', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'For the **17-year-old boy with a traumatic hyphaema** and an **elevated IOP** — what is the **best initial management**?',
    options:['Immediate surgery','Bed rest with 45° head elevation','Pilocarpine','Patching both eyes'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints **\"In the previous Q…\"**, and the previous Q is `opqb-t18-729`. Its answer has already been given, so the diagnosis may be named here.)*\n\n**`L25) Trauma I` slide 28 lists the treatment of hyphaema and the second line is the answer, with the angle printed:**\n\n> Hospitalization: **NOT EVERY CASE** (children, sickle cell disease, uncontrolled high IOP, coagulation or bleeding disorders) · **Bed rest (45 degree)** · Cycloplegics (**atropine**) · Topical steroid · **Antiglaucoma if increased IOP** · Surgical evacuation: persistent elevated IOP · total hyphaema > 5 days\n\n**⚠️ WHY 45 DEGREES AND NOT FLAT.** Sitting the patient up lets gravity keep the blood layered **inferiorly**, below the visual axis and below the pupil. Lying supine lets it disperse across the whole chamber, where it coats the endothelium, blocks the axis and — with a raised pressure — soaks into the stroma as **corneal blood staining** (`L25` slide 27, and `opqb-t18-742`). The posture is treatment, not comfort. **This is exactly why `opqb-t18-753` keys \"bed rest in SUPINE position\" as the measure that is NOT used.**\n\n**⚠️ AND THE REAL DANGER IS ON DAY 3 TO 5, NOT DAY 1.** `L25` slide 27 puts **RE-BLEEDING, common in the FIRST 5 DAYS** at the head of the complications, above the raised IOP. The initial bleed usually clears; the secondary bleed is bigger, fills the chamber and takes the pressure up. Everything in the regimen — rest, an upright head, atropine to stop the pupil moving, steroid to quieten the inflammation — is aimed at not disturbing the clot while it organises.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Immediate surgery** | Surgery is in the deck, but with **two specific triggers** and neither is present on day one: **persistently elevated IOP** and **a total hyphaema lasting more than 5 days**. This boy has a fluid level, not a total hyphaema, and a pressure that has not yet been treated medically. Operating into a fresh clot invites the re-bleed the whole regimen exists to prevent. |\n| **Pilocarpine** | ⚠️ **The exact opposite of what the deck prescribes, and the best distractor on the page.** `L25` gives **cycloplegia with ATROPINE**. Pilocarpine is a miotic: it constricts the pupil, pulls on the torn iris root that is the source of the bleeding, and worsens the inflammation. Atropine paralyses the iris and ciliary body, splints the tear and relieves the ciliary spasm. **A hyphaema is dilated, not constricted.** *(The rationale for atropine over pilocarpine is __not taken from the course material__; the choice of atropine is the slide’s.)* |\n| **Patching both eyes** | Binocular patching is an old idea for immobilising the eyes, and neither trauma deck offers it for hyphaema — patching appears only under **chemical injury** (`L25` slide 11). ⚠️ It is also the wrong instinct here: the eye needs to be **observed daily** for a re-bleed and a pressure rise, and a patch hides it. |\n\n**⚠️ And note who this patient is.** He is **17** — `L25` slide 28 admits **children**, and the same slide admits **uncontrolled high IOP**, which the stem gives. Whether he is admitted is a judgement about both; what he gets either way is the 45-degree bed rest.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A traumatic hyphaema is managed by bed rest at 45 degrees so the blood layers below the axis, with atropine and topical steroid; surgery waits for a persistent pressure or a total hyphaema beyond five days, and a miotic is the wrong drug.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q730), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-731', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient **struck with a cricket ball** shows **retinal whitening and a cherry-red spot**. Most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['CRAO','Commotio retinae','Retinal detachment','Vitreous hemorrhage'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slides 35–36 are titled \"Blunt trauma: COMMOTIO RETINAE (BERLIN’S EDEMA)\" and the fundus photograph carries exactly two burned-in labels: CHERRY-RED SPOT · RETINAL EDEMA.** The stem is those two labels plus the blunt blow that produces them.\n\n**⚠️ THE HISTORY IS THE DISCRIMINATOR, NOT THE FUNDUS.** A cherry-red spot with retinal whitening is also the picture of a **central retinal artery occlusion** — `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` case 1 is a 70-year-old hypertensive with **\"white opacified retina, CHERRY-RED MACULAR SPOT\"** and a right RAPD. **The two fundi look alike because the mechanism of the red spot is the same in both: the whitened, opacified retina surrounds a fovea that has no inner retina to whiten, so the intact choroidal circulation shows through as red.** What separates them is what came first:\n\n| | Commotio retinae | CRAO |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Preceding event** | **a blunt blow** — cricket ball, fist, tennis ball | an **embolus**, in an elderly vasculopath |\n| **Onset** | at the moment of impact | sudden, painless, over seconds |\n| **Acuity** | variable, often moderately reduced | **hand motion or worse**, below 6/60 |\n| **RAPD** | usually absent or mild | **present** |\n| **Course** | **usually resolves spontaneously** | **does not** |\n\n**⚠️ And commotio is not true oedema, despite the eponym.** The whitening is **disruption of the photoreceptor outer segments** by the concussive wave, not fluid — which is why it clears without treatment and why it is called *commotio*, a shaking. *(The photoreceptor mechanism and the spontaneous course are __not taken from the course material__ — `L25` supplies only the two photograph labels; both are recorded in `rd-9`.)*\n\n**Why the other two:**\n\n| Option | What the fundus would show instead |\n|---|---|\n| **Retinal detachment** | `L25` slides 37–38 pair **RETINAL TEAR** with **RETINAL DETACHMENT** as separate blunt-trauma lesions. A detachment is a **mobile, elevated, grey-white convex sheet** with the vessels running over its surface, and its symptoms are the flashes, floaters and curtain of `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations`. No cherry-red spot. |\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage** | Blood **in front of** the retina. It obscures the fundus rather than colouring it — `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 51 gives a dense vitreous haemorrhage an **ABSENT (black) red reflex**. If you can see a cherry-red spot at all, the vitreous is clear. |\n\n**⚠️ Why it still matters when it resolves by itself.** The blow that produced the commotio also produced whatever else `L25` slides 37–39 list — **retinal breaks, detachment, choroidal rupture** — and a break made today can detach months from now. **The eye is re-examined weeks later**, which is the point `rd-9` makes.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret` “Retinal detachment” — `rd-9` is the blunt-trauma retinal lesion trio.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Retinal whitening with a cherry-red spot immediately after a blunt blow is commotio retinae — Berlin’s oedema — and it is separated from a CRAO by the history, the acuity and the fact that it recovers.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q731), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-732', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'In the patient **struck with a cricket ball** whose fundus shows the **retinal whitening and cherry-red spot of commotio retinae** — what is **another finding in this condition**?',
    options:['Intraretinal hemorrhages','Iris prolapse','Lens dislocation only','Optic disc swelling'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints **\"In the previous Q…\"**, and the previous Q is `opqb-t18-731`. Its answer has already been given, so the diagnosis may be named.)*\n\n**The concussive wave that disrupts the photoreceptors also ruptures small retinal capillaries, and the blood stays where the capillaries are — inside the retina.** Intraretinal haemorrhages therefore accompany the whitening. *(The capillary rupture is __not taken from the course material__; `L25) Trauma I` slides 35–36 supply only the two labels CHERRY-RED SPOT and RETINAL EDEMA.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE WORD THAT MAKES THIS ANSWERABLE IS \"INTRARETINAL\", AND IT IS AN ANATOMICAL CLAIM.** The module uses the same discriminator elsewhere: `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` distinguishes **intraretinal** lesions — dot-and-blot haemorrhages, IRMA — from anything that reaches the vitreous, and `opqb-t19-764` turns on exactly that distinction. Here it means the bleeding belongs to the same layer as the injury, which is why it is a finding *of this condition* rather than a separate lesion.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | What it actually belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Iris prolapse** | ⚠️ **A sign of an OPEN GLOBE, and the wrong injury entirely.** Iris coming through a wound means the eye wall is breached — `L25` slides 43–45, the rupture/laceration/open-globe sequence, and the **peaked pupil** of slides 49–50. Commotio retinae is a **closed-globe** injury: the wall is intact and the damage is a shock wave transmitted through it. If this patient had iris prolapse you would be shielding the eye and calling a surgeon (`opqb-t18-736`), not describing his macula. |\n| **Lens dislocation only** | ⚠️ **The trap is the word \"only\".** Lens subluxation *is* a genuine blunt-trauma lesion — `L25` slide 34, and it gives uniocular diplopia — but it is a **separate lesion of the same blow**, not a feature of commotio retinae, and \"only\" makes the option exclusive of everything else. A blunt injury produces a *list* (`trm-7`), not one item. |\n| **Optic disc swelling** | Not part of the picture. In commotio the disc is normal — the injury is at the level of the outer retina. ⚠️ Disc swelling after trauma would point somewhere else altogether: raised intracranial pressure after a head injury, or the compressive picture of `opqb-t18-727`. And note that in **traumatic optic neuropathy** (`opqb-t18-721`) the disc is normal too on day one; it goes **pale**, weeks later, not swollen. |\n\n**⚠️ The examination point.** Because the whitening resolves, the haemorrhages resolve, and the acuity often comes back, the temptation is to discharge the patient. **What must not be missed is what else the same blow did** — `L25` slides 37–39 give retinal break, detachment and choroidal rupture, and the last of those (`opqb-t10-417`) permanently costs vision if it underlies the fovea.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret` “Retinal detachment” — `rd-9` carries the blunt-trauma retinal lesions.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Commotio retinae carries intraretinal haemorrhages alongside the whitening, because the same concussive wave ruptures retinal capillaries; iris prolapse belongs to an open globe and lens dislocation is a separate lesion of the same blow.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.116 (Q732), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-733', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **25-year-old after trauma** shows an **irregular lens edge shimmering with movement**. What **ring** may be seen on the **lens surface**?',
    options:['Kayser-Fleischer ring','Vossius ring','Weiss ring','Sampaolesi line'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A Vossius ring is a ring of PIGMENT imprinted on the ANTERIOR LENS CAPSULE by the pupil margin.** When a blunt blow drives the iris back against the lens, the posterior pigment epithelium of the pupillary margin is stamped onto the capsule and stays there — a brown annulus roughly the diameter the pupil had at the moment of impact.\n\n**⚠️ A SPELLING TRAP THAT HIDES HALF THE MODULE.** `L25) Trauma I` slides 30–31 print it **\"Vossious\"**; `L18) Cataract II` prints it **\"Vossius\"**. `Vossius` returns hits **only** in `L18` and `Vossious` **only** in `L25` — a word-grep on either spelling alone finds one deck and misses the other. `L18` tabulates traumatic cataract with **\"Vossius ring (blunt)\"** in its signs column and **\"Rosette (blunt), diffuse (penetrating)\"** in its morphology column.\n\n**⚠️ THE STEM DESCRIBES A DIFFERENT LESION FROM THE ONE IT ASKS ABOUT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** *An irregular lens edge shimmering with movement* is **subluxation** — the zonules are partly torn, the displaced lens edge crosses the pupil, and the lens wobbles (**phacodonesis**) with eye movement. That is `L25` slide 34, and it is what `opqb-t18-752` asks about. **The question is: given a lens that has clearly taken a blunt blow, what else is on its surface?** Both are lesions of the same injury.\n\n**Why the other three — and each is a real sign in a different place:**\n\n| Option | Where it actually is, and what it means |\n|---|---|\n| **Kayser-Fleischer ring** | **CORNEA, not lens.** A ring of copper in **Descemet’s membrane** at the limbus, the ocular sign of **Wilson’s disease**. ⚠️ Copper is the connection the bank is exploiting — the copper *foreign body* of `L25` slides 46–47 gives **chalcosis**, and its lens sign is the **sunflower cataract** (`L17) Cataract I`, and `cat-10`). Wilson’s copper and a copper IOFB produce related deposits by entirely different routes. *(Wilson’s disease is named by no cached deck — __not taken from the course material__.)* |\n| **Weiss ring** | **VITREOUS, not lens.** The glial annulus torn from the optic disc margin when the posterior hyaloid separates — the marker of a **posterior vitreous detachment**. ⚠️ MEASURED: `Weiss ring` returns **exactly one hit across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters, and it is inside `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations`’ own NOT-COVERED list** — the module genuinely does not teach it. |\n| **Sampaolesi line** | **ANGLE, not lens, and a LINE rather than a ring** — the stem asks for a ring. It is a band of pigment anterior to Schwalbe’s line, seen on gonioscopy in **pigment dispersion and pseudoexfoliation**. *(Named by no cached deck — __not taken from the course material__.)* |\n\n**⚠️ The Vossius ring is harmless in itself** — it does not affect vision and it fades over months. **Its value is as a witness**: it proves the eye took a blunt blow hard enough to drive iris onto lens, which means the rest of `trm-7`’s list must be looked for.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — `trm-7` carries the blunt-trauma lesion list front to back; the ring itself is written in `cat-10`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A Vossius ring is pigment stamped onto the anterior lens capsule by the pupil margin in blunt trauma; the Kayser–Fleischer ring is corneal, the Weiss ring vitreous and the Sampaolesi line angular.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q733), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-734', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Which is the **most urgent ophthalmic emergency**?',
    options:['CRAO','Chemical burn','Retinal detachment','Hyphema'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slide 2 opens the entire lecture with this sentence, printed in red:**\n\n> **They are the MOST URGENT ophthalmic emergency.**\n> They require **emergency management by the FIRST PERSON WHO SEES the affected patient.**\n\n**The second line is what \"most urgent\" means here, and it is what separates a chemical burn from the other three.** Every other emergency on this list is treated by an ophthalmologist. A chemical burn is treated by whoever is standing there — a bystander, a factory nurse, a casualty officer — **with tap water, before anyone qualified arrives**. There is no other ocular condition for which the correct action is taken by an untrained person in the first thirty seconds.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE URGENCY IS MEASURED IN SECONDS, NOT MINUTES.** The bank’s own printed box at `opqb-t18-716` says it: **\"the speed and efficacy of irrigation is the MOST IMPORTANT PROGNOSTIC FACTOR following chemical injury.\"** The chemical is still reacting while you decide. `L25` slide 4 adds that an alkali **\"continues penetration for WEEKS\"** — so every second of delay is corneal stroma that will not be recovered.\n\n**The other three are emergencies. They are just not this one:**\n\n| Option | Its own time window, and who acts |\n|---|---|\n| **CRAO** | ⚠️ **The genuine competitor, and the reason this question is not trivial.** A central retinal artery occlusion infarcts the inner retina within hours and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` gives it as case 1. **But nothing a first responder does changes it** — and the module is blunt about how little anyone can do: all four of `L31,32`’s Management slides are **empty**, the word alone on white. It is desperately urgent and largely untreatable. |\n| **Retinal detachment** | Urgent in **days**, and the urgency is graded by whether the **macula is still on**. `L20)` teaches it as a surgical booking, not a same-hour emergency. |\n| **Hyphema** | Managed by **bed rest at 45°**, atropine and observation (`L25` slide 28). Its danger — the **re-bleed of the first 5 days** — is a reason for follow-up, not for immediate action. Even `L25` says hospitalisation is **\"NOT EVERY CASE\"**. |\n\n**⚠️ The exam habit to break.** \"Sudden painless total loss of vision\" *feels* more urgent than a painful red eye, so CRAO is the intuitive answer. **The module ranks by how much the first minute changes the outcome**, and by that measure the chemical burn wins outright: irrigation started in thirty seconds and irrigation started in ten minutes give different eyes.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-acute` “Acute Visual Loss” — the CRAO distractor is `L31,32`’s case 1.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chemical injury is the module’s most urgent ophthalmic emergency because the first person on the scene must irrigate before anyone qualified arrives, and speed of irrigation is the single most important prognostic factor.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q734), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-735', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**Hypopyon in trauma** indicates:',
    options:['Viral infection','Endophthalmitis','Allergic conjunctivitis','Retinal dialysis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A hypopyon is a layer of PUS in the anterior chamber, and in an eye that has just been injured, pus means the eye is infected.**\n\n`L37) Red Eye` defines it in its examination sequence — *\"detect any layered blood or pus, which would indicate either hyphema or hypopyon, respectively\"* — and Fig 4-6 shows **\"a prominent layering of purulent material… in the inferior aspect of the anterior chamber.\"** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.41** lists **hyphaema and hypopyon** side by side among the anterior chamber findings, with photographs captioned *Blood (Hyphema)* and *Pus (Hypopyon)*.\n\n**⚠️ WHY TRAUMA MAKES IT AN INFECTION AND NOT AN INFLAMMATION.** A sterile hypopyon does occur — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 16 gives anterior uveitis the signs **\"Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± HYPOPYON\"**, and `opqb-t10-428` offers hypopyon as a distractor in an HLA-B27 uveitis vignette (it keys **miosis**, because the hypopyon is a late sign there and the question asks for an early one). But the stem says *in trauma*, and a traumatised eye has a route in. `L25) Trauma I` slide 52 states the link that makes this question answerable: **\"the presence of an intraocular foreign body (IOFB) suggests globe PENETRATION.\"** An open globe is an inoculated globe. **Post-traumatic endophthalmitis is the feared end point of every open-globe injury**, which is why `L25` slide 51 forbids drops, patches and pressure and sends the patient to theatre.\n\n**The sign to hold onto:** `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 52 lists the causes of a **yellow/white pupillary reflex** as **retinoblastoma** and **\"pus in the vitreous (endophthalmitis)\"** — so pus that has reached the vitreous is visible in the red reflex, and pus that has reached the anterior chamber is visible as a hypopyon. Either is the same diagnosis.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Viral infection** | Viral ocular disease in this module is a **surface** disease — adenoviral conjunctivitis, herpes simplex dendritic keratitis, herpes zoster ophthalmicus (`cnj-8`, `cor-5`, `cor-6`). ⚠️ Viruses do not generate a neutrophil layer in the anterior chamber. |\n| **Allergic conjunctivitis** | Type I hypersensitivity of the conjunctiva — itching, papillae, watery discharge (`cnj-5`, `cnj-6`). **It does not enter the eye at all.** A hypopyon means the anterior chamber is involved, and allergy never is. |\n| **Retinal dialysis** | A **retinal break** at the ora serrata — the classically traumatic break, and a cause of detachment. ⚠️ It is offered because it is a genuine blunt-trauma lesion and because the word *dialysis* sounds like a fluid. It is a tear, it is posterior, and it produces no pus. |\n\n**⚠️ The clinical instruction.** A hypopyon in a recently injured eye is not observed and it is not steroided as if it were uveitis — it is **cultured and treated as endophthalmitis**, urgently. `L18) Cataract II` gives the module’s only regimen for it, in the post-operative setting: **intravitreal vancomycin** plus a second agent.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-orbit-mass` “Orbital emergencies, tumours and the socket” — `orb-12` separates endophthalmitis from panophthalmitis and cellulitis.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A hypopyon is layered pus, so in an eye that has just been injured it means the globe has been breached and infected — post-traumatic endophthalmitis, not a viral, allergic or retinal cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q735), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-736', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **27-year-old man** was hit in the eye with a **sharp metal wire**. His **vision is severely reduced**. There is **corneal irregularity and a shallow anterior chamber**. You **suspect an open globe**. What is the **MOST appropriate first action**?',
    options:['Check IOP','Apply pressure bandage','Place a rigid protective eye shield','Instill topical steroids'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slide 51 is titled \"If you suspect an open globe or globe is ruptured\" and its first line is the answer:**\n\n> **Place EYE SHIELD & CALL for OPHTHALMOLOGIST immediately**\n> **DO NOT PLACE A PATCH** as pressure can extrude intraocular contents\n> **DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE**\n> No further eye exam or eye drops or ointment on an open globe\n> Instruct the patient to **withhold food and fluids (NPO)** preparing for GA\n\n**⚠️ THE WORD \"RIGID\" IS THE WHOLE ANSWER, AND IT IS WHAT SEPARATES OPTION C FROM OPTION B.** A shield is a hard dome that rests on the **bony orbital rim** and touches the eye nowhere. A patch or bandage presses on the lids, the lids press on the globe, and a globe with a hole in it does not resist pressure — **the vitreous, the lens and the uvea come out through the wound and do not go back**. `L25` says this in as many words: *pressure can extrude intraocular contents.* Same intention, opposite result.\n\n**The stem is a checklist of open-globe signs.** `L25` slides 49–50 list what to look for: **peaked pupil · shallow OR deep anterior chamber · bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage · a positive SEIDEL TEST**, in a patient with a **suspicious history — e.g. HAMMERING**. This patient has a **sharp metal wire** (the mechanism), **corneal irregularity** (the wound) and a **shallow AC** (aqueous has leaked out). That is enough. `L25` slide 49 is emphatic about the order of operations: an open globe is the **\"MOST DEVASTATING of all eye injuries\"** and must be **\"RULED OUT BEFORE detailed history and focused exam.\"**\n\n**Why the other three — and all three are on the deck’s forbidden list:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Check IOP** | ⚠️ **Explicitly forbidden: \"DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE.\"** Applanation tonometry presses the cornea. On an open globe that is the same mechanical insult as the bandage, applied through a smaller area. This is what `opqb-t18-737` asks next. |\n| **Apply pressure bandage** | ⚠️ **Explicitly forbidden, and the most instinctive wrong answer in trauma** — pressure is what you apply to a bleeding limb. It is the exact opposite of what an open eye needs. |\n| **Instill topical steroids** | ⚠️ Forbidden by the same line: **\"no further eye exam or eye drops or ointment on an open globe.\"** Two reasons — drops are **not sterile once the globe is open** and drive surface organisms into the eye, and the bottle tip touching the lids can itself press. *(The contamination route is __not taken from the course material__; the prohibition is the slide’s.)* |\n\n**⚠️ AND THE INSTRUCTION THAT IS NOT ON THE OPTION LIST MATTERS TOO: NIL BY MOUTH.** `L25` slide 51 ends with it, because the eye is going to theatre under **general anaesthesia** — a patient who is given a cup of tea in casualty delays the repair by six hours.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cornea` “Cornea — infection, trauma and the red eye” — the Seidel test that confirms the leak is `cor-2` and `opmcq-c8-17`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A suspected open globe gets a rigid shield resting on the orbital rim and an immediate call to an ophthalmologist — never a pressure bandage, never tonometry and never drops, because any pressure can extrude the eye’s contents.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q736), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-737', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'In the **27-year-old struck with a sharp metal wire**, with **corneal irregularity, a shallow anterior chamber and a suspected open globe** — which of the following **SHOULD NOT be done**?',
    options:['Measure IOP','Record visual acuity','Assess for other body trauma','Start IV antibiotics'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(Back-reference repaired: the bank prints **\"In the previous Q…\"**, and the previous Q is `opqb-t18-736`. The restatement stops at *suspected open globe* — `opqb-t18-736`’s answer, the shield, is deliberately **not** carried across.)*\n\n**`L25) Trauma I` slide 51 prints one prohibition per line and this is the second of them: \"DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE.\"**\n\n**Why tonometry is the one examination that must be refused.** Every method of measuring IOP works by pushing on the eye. Applanation flattens a disc of cornea against a prism; indentation dents it; even a rebound probe strikes it. On an intact globe the eye pushes back and the reading is the pressure. **On an open globe there is nothing to push back** — the force is transmitted straight to the contents, and the vitreous, lens or uvea follows the path of least resistance out through the wound. `L25` gives the reason under the adjacent line: **\"pressure can extrude intraocular contents.\"** ⚠️ **And the number would be worthless anyway:** an open globe is hypotonous by definition, so the measurement answers a question nobody needs answered while risking the eye to obtain it.\n\n**Why the other three are all correct things to do:**\n\n| Option | Why it is done |\n|---|---|\n| **Record visual acuity** | ⚠️ **The most valuable single number in ocular trauma, and it costs nothing.** `L25` slide 59 puts **visual acuity** first in step 2 of the focused examination. It is the baseline against which everything afterwards is judged, and it is medico-legally essential. It is taken **without touching the eye** — chart, or counting fingers, or hand movements, or light perception. |\n| **Assess for other body trauma** | ⚠️ **The point of the whole question, and the one students skip.** A wire injury at speed rarely arrives alone. `L25` slide 56’s focused history opens with the **mechanism of injury** and the timing precisely because the mechanism predicts what else was hit. An eye is not treated in isolation from an airway. |\n| **Start IV antibiotics** | Prophylaxis against **post-traumatic endophthalmitis**, which is what `opqb-t18-735` is about and what makes an open globe so dangerous. ⚠️ Note the route: **systemic** antibiotics are given, **topical** ones are forbidden by the same slide — *no eye drops or ointment on an open globe*. *(The systemic-versus-topical distinction is __not taken from the course material__; neither trauma deck prescribes an antibiotic for the open globe, and `L25` says only \"call for ophthalmologist\".)* |\n\n**⚠️ Note that option A here is the SAME action as option A in `opqb-t18-736`.** The bank has printed a deliberate pair: one question asks what to do, the other asks what not to do, and *checking the pressure* is the wrong answer to both. **That is not a duplicate — it is the same rule tested from opposite sides**, and neither question’s options overlap otherwise.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Tonometry is forbidden in a suspected open globe because any applied pressure can extrude the eye’s contents; recording acuity, assessing for other injuries and giving systemic antibiotics are all part of the correct workup.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q737), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-738', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'What complication may occur due to an **iron intraocular foreign body**?',
    options:['Chalcosis','Siderosis bulbi','Band keratopathy','Keratoconus'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slides 46–47 print the whole answer as a three-line list, pairing each metal with its own disease:**\n\n| Material | What it produces |\n|---|---|\n| **Iron** | **SIDEROSIS** |\n| **Copper** | **CHALCOSIS** |\n| **Inert** | stone, glass, **pastic** [sic], wood, … |\n\n**Siderosis bulbi is iron poisoning of the eye.** A retained ferrous fragment dissolves slowly in the ocular fluids and iron is taken up by epithelial tissues throughout the eye — lens epithelium, iris, ciliary epithelium and retina. The clinical consequences are a **rusty-brown iris that turns the eye heterochromic**, a **mid-dilated poorly reactive pupil**, a **siderotic cataract** with brown deposits under the anterior capsule, and — the one that ends the eye — a **pigmentary retinopathy with progressive loss of the ERG**. *(The tissue distribution, the ERG and the individual signs are __not taken from the course material__; `L25` supplies the word SIDEROSIS and its cause.)*\n\n**⚠️ WHICH IS WHY `opqb-t18-718` KEYS \"MUST BE REMOVED\".** The two questions are the same fact stated as a consequence and as an instruction. Iron is never left, whatever its size, because siderosis is silent until it is irreversible.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **Chalcosis** | ⚠️ **The paired answer, and the reason this question works.** It is the **COPPER** disease from the same slide — copper deposits give a **sunflower cataract** (`L17) Cataract I`’s name for the traumatic morphology, recorded in `cat-10`) and a **Kayser–Fleischer-like ring** in Descemet’s membrane. Same slide, wrong metal. |\n| **Band keratopathy** | Calcium in **Bowman’s layer**, in the interpalpebral band. `L15,16) Cornea` and `ophthalmology.pdf` chapter 10 give it as a complication of **chronic anterior uveitis** — it is `uv-7`’s territory — and of chronic hypercalcaemia. Not a metal, not a foreign body, and it is calcium rather than iron. |\n| **Keratoconus** | A **corneal ectasia**, progressive thinning and conical protrusion — `op-cornea-surg`’s chapter title names it (*\"Cornea — ectasia, grafts and refractive surgery\"*). It is a developmental/biomechanical disease with no traumatic cause in this module. |\n\n**⚠️ The examination habit:** *iron → siderosis, copper → chalcosis* is a two-line pairing and the bank tests it by offering the other half of the pair as the distractor. Learn the pair, not the item.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A retained iron intraocular foreign body causes siderosis bulbi; chalcosis is the copper disease, band keratopathy is calcium in Bowman’s layer and keratoconus is an ectasia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q738), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-739', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**MRI is contraindicated** when:',
    options:['Wooden IOFB suspected','Glass IOFB suspected','Metallic IOFB suspected','Intraocular tumor suspected'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slide 52 prints it as an instruction in capitals: \"DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED.\"**\n\nThe scanner’s static field exerts **translational force and torque** on a ferromagnetic fragment. A splinter of steel lying quietly against the retina can be pulled or twisted inside the bore, cutting through whatever lies in its path — retina, lens, optic nerve. *(The physics is __not taken from the course material__; the prohibition is the slide’s.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE PROHIBITION IS ON SUSPICION, NOT ON PROOF.** The slide says *if a metallic IOFB is SUSPECTED* — you do not have to have seen it. That is why the history matters: `L25` slide 49 gives **HAMMERING** as the archetypal suspicious mechanism, and grinding, drilling and lathe work are the same. **The scan that answers the question safely is CT**, which the same slide asks for by name: *\"determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB\"* and *\"show the shape and integrity of the traumatized globe.\"*\n\n**Why the other three are not contraindications:**\n\n| Option | Why MRI is safe, or even preferred |\n|---|---|\n| **Wooden IOFB** | ⚠️ **The inverse case, and worth knowing for its own sake.** Wood is one of `L25` slides 46–47’s **inert** materials — non-magnetic, so no force. And it is **radiolucent**: dry wood can be missed on CT or mistaken for air, so **MRI is the better scan for organic material**, not the forbidden one. *(The radiolucency of wood is __not taken from the course material__.)* |\n| **Glass IOFB** | Also on the **inert** list. Glass is non-magnetic, so the field does nothing to it — and it is radio-opaque enough to be seen on CT anyway. |\n| **Intraocular tumor** | The opposite of a contraindication. MRI is a soft-tissue modality and is used to characterise orbital and intraocular masses — `L10) orbit` uses it for eccentric proptosis and orbital lesions. **Nothing about a tumour interacts with a magnetic field.** |\n\n**⚠️ Hold this with `opqb-t18-717`, which tests the same rule from the other direction** — *which test is NOT useful for a retained IRON foreign body* — and note that the two questions share **not one option string**: one enumerates modalities, the other enumerates foreign-body materials. **They are two questions about one rule, not a reprint.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'MRI is contraindicated on suspicion of a metallic intraocular foreign body, because the field can move a ferromagnetic fragment through the eye; wood and glass are inert and a tumour is an indication rather than a contraindication.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q739), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-740', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Which findings are expected on the **SAME DAY** of blunt ocular trauma?',
    options:['Blood staining of the cornea','Hyphaema','Hypopyon'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**⚠️ THIS QUESTION IS PRINTED WITH ONLY THREE OPTIONS — A, B and C. There is no option D on the page**; the space beneath \"C. Hypopyon\" is blank paper and the next printed line is the number 741. Verified at 300 dpi. Recorded, not corrected.\n\n**The question is entirely about TIMING, and each of the three options belongs to a different point on the clock.**\n\n| Finding | When it appears, and why |\n|---|---|\n| **Hyphaema** | **IMMEDIATELY.** A blunt blow shears the vessels at the **iris root** and blood enters the anterior chamber at the moment of impact — `opqb-t18-729`’s printed box: *\"the source of bleeding is the iris root… the blood settles inferiorly with a resultant fluid level.\"* Nothing has to happen first. |\n| **Blood staining of the cornea** | **DAYS.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 27 lists it as a **COMPLICATION** of hyphaema, under **\"uncontrolled high IOP\"** alongside optic nerve damage and CRAO. It requires a hyphaema to exist, then a raised pressure to be sustained, then haemoglobin breakdown products to be driven into an endothelium-damaged stroma. **It is the consequence of a hyphaema, so it cannot be same-day.** ⚠️ It is also what `opqb-t18-742` keys as the most feared complication. |\n| **Hypopyon** | **DAYS.** Pus is a cellular response — organisms have to enter, multiply and recruit neutrophils. `opqb-t18-735` keys it as the sign of **endophthalmitis** after a penetrating injury. And note the stem says **blunt** trauma: a closed-globe injury has no route of entry at all, so a hypopyon here would be doubly wrong. |\n\n**⚠️ THE SEQUENCE IS THE THING TO CARRY AWAY, AND IT IS WHY `L25` SLIDE 27 EXISTS.** Day 0 the eye bleeds. Days 1–5 it may **re-bleed** — *\"RE-BLEEDING common in the FIRST 5 DAYS\"* — and if the pressure is not controlled the cornea stains. **That is the whole reason for the 45-degree bed rest of `opqb-t18-730`:** the posture is protecting a cornea that will not stain for another three days.\n\n**⚠️ And the bank’s spelling wobbles inside its own topic:** it prints **\"Hyphaema\"** here and **\"Hyphema\"** at `opqb-t18-734` and `opqb-t18-753`. Both spellings are in circulation; the same word is meant.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A hyphaema is present the moment a blunt injury tears the iris root; corneal blood staining and hypopyon are both later consequences — one of sustained raised pressure, the other of infection through a breached globe.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q740), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-741', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient has **significant visual loss after blunt trauma**. What condition could explain this?',
    options:["Berlin's edema",'Petroplon avese','Conjunctival abrasion','Ecchymosis of the lid'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Berlin’s oedema is `L25) Trauma I`’s own name for COMMOTIO RETINAE, and it is the only option on this list that sits in the visual axis.**\n\nSlides 35–36 are titled **\"Blunt trauma: Commotio retinae (BERLIN’S EDEMA)\"** and the fundus photograph carries two burned-in labels: **CHERRY-RED SPOT · RETINAL EDEMA**. The concussive wave from a blunt blow is transmitted through the vitreous to the posterior pole and disrupts the **photoreceptor outer segments**, so the retina whitens and vision drops. *(The photoreceptor mechanism, and the fact that it usually recovers, are __not taken from the course material__ — `L25` supplies only the two labels; both are recorded in `rd-9`.)*\n\n**⚠️ THE QUESTION IS A SORTING EXERCISE: WHICH LESIONS ARE ON THE VISUAL AXIS AND WHICH ARE COSMETIC.** Blunt trauma produces a long list (`trm-7`) and most of it does not touch vision at all. The two obviously superficial options are here to be discarded:\n\n| Option | Where it is, and what it costs |\n|---|---|\n| **Conjunctival abrasion** | A surface epithelial defect. **Painful, gritty, red — and the conjunctiva is not in the optical path.** ⚠️ Contrast a *corneal* abrasion (`cor-10`), which is on the axis and does blur vision. The bank has chosen the conjunctival one deliberately. |\n| **Ecchymosis of the lid** | The **black eye** of `L25` slide 17 — periorbital bruising, the commonest visible sign of a blunt blow and the one that alarms the patient most. **It is skin.** The eye behind it sees normally, which is exactly why a black eye must never be accepted as the explanation for lost vision — the `opqb-t18-727` patient looked like this too, and had an orbital compartment syndrome. |\n\n**⚠️ OPTION B IS A PRINTED NONSENSE STRING AND IT IS CARRIED HERE VERBATIM.** The bank prints **\"Petroplon avese\"** — two words that are not a medical term. It was read letter by letter at 300 dpi and that is exactly what is on the page. **MEASURED:** `Petroplon` returns **zero hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters**, and so does `Purtscher` — so it cannot be resolved against the course material, and it is not a mangling of anything this module teaches. It is reproduced rather than repaired because the app must show what the bank printed. **Treat it as an unreadable distractor; it is not the answer and it cannot be studied.**\n\n**⚠️ What ELSE could have explained the visual loss, had the bank offered it.** This is the more useful list, and the topic tests most of it elsewhere: **hyphaema** filling the axis (`opqb-t18-729`), **traumatic cataract** (`opqb-t18-744`), **lens subluxation** (`opqb-t18-752`), **vitreous haemorrhage**, **retinal break with detachment** (`L25` slides 37–38), **choroidal rupture under the fovea** (`opqb-t10-417`), **traumatic optic neuropathy** (`opqb-t18-721`) and **retrobulbar haemorrhage** (`opqb-t18-727`). **Blunt trauma can take the vision from the cornea to the optic canal**, and the examination has to walk the whole path.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret` “Retinal detachment” — `rd-9` carries commotio retinae and its differential against a CRAO.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Berlin’s oedema — commotio retinae — is the posterior-pole lesion of blunt trauma and explains visual loss; a conjunctival abrasion and a lid ecchymosis are outside the optical path however dramatic they look.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q741), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-742', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **5-year-old child** had a **blunt ocular trauma a few days ago** and presents with a **red eye and defective vision**. Examination reveals a **dark red coagulum in the anterior chamber**. The **most feared complication** for this condition is:',
    options:['The affection of the cornea.','The affection of the retina.','The affection of the lens.','The affection of the pupil.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The dark red coagulum is a total hyphaema — the bank’s own \"eight-ball\" — and the complication it fears is BLOOD STAINING OF THE CORNEA.**\n\n`L25) Trauma I` slide 27 lists the complications of hyphaema and puts corneal staining inside the pressure branch:\n\n> **RE-BLEEDING common in the FIRST 5 DAYS**\n> **Uncontrolled high IOP** which can cause: optic nerve damage · **BLOOD STAINING OF THE CORNEA** · CRAO\n\n**⚠️ EVERY DETAIL IN THE STEM IS AIMED AT THIS ANSWER.** *Dark red* is `opqb-t18-729`’s printed box: **\"a total hyphemia with deoxygenation of the blood in the AC making it dark in color… Its presence carries a HIGHER RISK OF COMPLICATIONS.\"** *A few days ago* puts the eye past the first bleed and into the window where the pressure has been up long enough to matter. *Defective vision* says the axis is already involved.\n\n**How the cornea stains.** A chamber packed with clot blocks the trabecular meshwork, the pressure rises, and haemoglobin breakdown products are driven forward into a stroma whose **endothelial pump has been damaged by the same pressure**. The cornea turns a rusty greenish-brown, from the periphery inward. *(The endothelial mechanism is __not taken from the course material__; the complication and its cause are the slide’s.)*\n\n**⚠️ AND THIS IS WHY THE CHILD IS ADMITTED.** `L25` slide 28: hospitalisation is **\"NOT EVERY CASE (CHILDREN, sickle cell disease, uncontrolled high IOP, coagulation or bleeding disorders)\"**. A five-year-old is on that list first — he cannot be relied on to rest at 45 degrees and cannot report a re-bleed.\n\n**⚠️ AND WHY IT IS *MOST FEARED* IN A FIVE-YEAR-OLD SPECIFICALLY.** A stained cornea in an adult clears slowly and the eye recovers. In a child under about seven the same months of blur cause **irreversible deprivation amblyopia** — the visual cortex does not wait. *(Amblyopia risk is __not taken from the course material__ in this context; `L18) Cataract II` states the principle for dense congenital cataract, *\"prevent amblyopia (within weeks)\"*.)*\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **The affection of the retina** | ⚠️ **The best distractor**, because `L25` slide 27 genuinely does list **CRAO** and **optic nerve damage** under the same raised pressure. But those are consequences of an *acutely* very high pressure, and the option says \"the retina\" where the deck’s posterior complications are vascular and neural. Corneal staining is the one the slide names as belonging to the cornea. |\n| **The affection of the lens** | A blunt blow can certainly damage the lens — rosette cataract, Vossius ring, subluxation (`trm-7`). **But none of those is a complication of the hyphaema**; they are separate lesions of the same original blow. The question asks what the *condition* leads to. |\n| **The affection of the pupil** | Traumatic mydriasis and sphincter tears occur, and iridodialysis distorts the pupil (`opqb-t18-751`). Again these are **direct** injuries, present from day one, not complications developing over days — and none of them is sight-threatening the way a stained cornea is. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A dark red total hyphaema days after blunt trauma threatens blood staining of the cornea through sustained raised pressure — and in a five-year-old that opacity costs vision permanently through amblyopia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.117 (Q742), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-743', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-vasc',
    stem:'A **cherry-red spot** is present in all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Tay–Sachs disease','Commotio retinae','Central retinal artery occlusion','Central retinal vein occlusion'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A cherry-red spot is not a lesion — it is a CONTRAST EFFECT, and it needs the retina AROUND the fovea to turn white.**\n\nThe fovea has no inner retinal layers and is the thinnest part of the retina, so the choroidal circulation shows through it as red. **Whiten everything else and the untouched fovea stands out as a red dot.** That single mechanism explains all three conditions that do produce it, and it also explains why the fourth cannot.\n\n| Condition | How the surrounding retina turns white |\n|---|---|\n| **Central retinal artery occlusion** | Inner retinal **infarction**. `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` case 1: **\"white opacified retina, CHERRY-RED MACULAR SPOT\"** in a 70-year-old hypertensive with hand-motion vision and an RAPD. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` prints the same sign. |\n| **Commotio retinae** | **Concussive whitening** of the outer retina after a blunt blow. `L25) Trauma I` slides 35–36 label the fundus photograph **CHERRY-RED SPOT · RETINAL EDEMA** — this topic’s `opqb-t18-731`. |\n| **Tay–Sachs disease** | **Ganglioside storage** in the retinal ganglion cells, which are packed everywhere except at the fovea where there are none — so the retina goes creamy-white around a red centre. ⚠️ **MEASURED: `Tay` returns ZERO hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters** — the storage diseases are outside this course entirely. __Not taken from the course material.__ |\n\n**⚠️ WHY A CENTRAL RETINAL VEIN OCCLUSION CANNOT PRODUCE ONE — AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE PICTURE IN EVERY RESPECT.** An arterial occlusion starves the retina, so it goes **pale**. A venous occlusion **dams** it, so it becomes engorged: `L19.2)` and `op-ret-vasc` give CRVO **flame and blot haemorrhages in all four quadrants, dilated tortuous veins, cotton-wool spots and a swollen disc** — the classic *blood and thunder* fundus. **The background is red, not white, so there is no contrast for a red fovea to stand against.** `opqb-t13-514` and `opqb-t13-554` test the same separation from the other side.\n\n**⚠️ The clinical use of the sign is that it means almost nothing on its own.** Three completely unrelated diseases produce an identical fundus appearance, and what separates them is entirely outside the eye: **a blunt blow** (commotio, recovers), **an embolus in an elderly vasculopath** (CRAO, does not recover), or **an infant with developmental regression** (Tay–Sachs). `rd-9` makes the first-versus-second point explicitly.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — commotio retinae is `trm-7` and this topic’s `opqb-t18-731`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A cherry-red spot appears whenever the retina around the fovea turns white — by infarction, by concussion or by storage — so it cannot occur in a central retinal vein occlusion, whose fundus is engorged and haemorrhagic.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q743), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-744', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'A **blunt trauma to the eye** commonly results in:',
    options:['Anterior subcapsular cataract','Posterior subcapsular cataract','Blue-dot cataract','Morgagnian cataract'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Two of the four options are excluded outright by `L17) Cataract I`, which sorts cataracts by cause and prints each morphology beside its own aetiology:**\n\n| `L17`’s line | What it excludes here |\n|---|---|\n| **\"Congenital, developmental → lamellar, BLUE DOT, anterior pyramidal, posterior polar, membranous, sutural\"** | **Blue-dot cataract is developmental** — present from childhood, not made by a blow. |\n| **\"Hypermature → … MORGAGNIAN (liquefaction of cortex and sinking of nucleus)\"** | **Morgagnian is the end stage of a SENILE cortical cataract**, a lens that has liquefied over years. |\n\n**What the decks DO print for blunt trauma is a shape, not a location.** `L25) Trauma I` slides 32–33 are titled **\"Blunt trauma: ROSETTE-SHAPED CATARACT\"** and show it in the red reflex; `L18) Cataract II` tabulates traumatic cataract as **\"Rosette (blunt), diffuse (penetrating)\"** with **\"Vossius ring (blunt)\"** in its signs column. ⚠️ **Neither trauma deck, and neither cataract deck, says where in the lens the rosette sits.** The choice between an anterior and a posterior subcapsular position is settled from outside the course.\n\n**⚠️ THE ROSETTE IS POSTERIOR SUBCAPSULAR, AND THE REASON IS MECHANICAL.** The classic traumatic rosette — feathery opacities radiating from the posterior pole along the lens sutures — forms **beneath the posterior capsule**, which is the thinnest part of the capsular bag and the part least able to resist the sudden equatorial stretch a blunt blow imposes. Zonular traction and capsular deformation disturb the underlying epithelium and cortical fibres there first. *(The posterior subcapsular location and its mechanism are __not taken from the course material__.)*\n\n**⚠️ AND THE MODULE PRINTS THE SAME ANSWER FOR A NEIGHBOURING CAUSE, WHICH IS THE USEFUL CROSS-CHECK.** `L17` gives **\"Thermal, Complicated → POSTERIOR SUBCAPSULAR cataract\"**, and `L18` gives complicated cataract **\"usually PSC\"** with **\"posterior synechiae (uveitis)\"** in its signs column. The posterior subcapsular region is where the lens registers insult from outside — heat, inflammation, steroid, radiation and concussion all land there.\n\n**Why not anterior subcapsular.** An anterior subcapsular opacity is what follows **direct anterior capsular contact or a chemical/heat insult from the front**, and in this module it is the pattern of the **Vossius ring’s** neighbour rather than of the concussion cataract. *(__Not taken from the course material__.)*\n\n**⚠️ Note the discrimination this topic sets up.** `opqb-t18-733` asks what is on the **surface** of the lens (Vossius ring, anterior capsule) and this asks what is **inside** it (the rosette). Same blow, two lesions, two questions four numbers apart.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — `trm-7`’s lesion list carries the rosette; the morphologies themselves are `cat-10`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The blunt-trauma cataract is the rosette, and it forms beneath the posterior capsule — the same subcapsular region that registers heat, inflammation and radiation; blue-dot is developmental and Morgagnian is hypermature senile.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q744), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-745', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient with a history of **blunt trauma to the left eye** complains of **double vision that disappears on covering the left eye and persists on covering the right eye**. Examination would prove one of the following conditions:',
    options:['A pupil showing the lens equator.','A miotic pupil.','An ectropion uveac','A Vossius ring.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Read the two clauses in the stem as a test, because that is what they are.**\n\n- *Disappears on covering the LEFT eye* → the left eye is the one producing the double image.\n- *Persists on covering the RIGHT eye* → **the left eye still sees double by itself.**\n\nThat second clause is the whole diagnosis. `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.42**, Table (1-3), defines it: **monocular diplopia is \"double vision in one eye\" that \"REMAINS EVEN AFTER OCCLUSION of the other eye\"**, against binocular diplopia which **\"disappears when any of the eyes are occluded.\"** **Double vision that survives covering the fellow eye is a problem INSIDE the eye — never a muscle and never a nerve.**\n\n**`L25) Trauma I` labels exactly two blunt-trauma lesions \"(UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\": IRIDODIALYSIS (slide 29) and SUBLUXATION of the lens (slide 34).** Only one of them is on this option list, and it is described rather than named: **\"a pupil showing the lens equator\"** is a subluxated lens. Partial zonular rupture lets the lens tilt and slip, so its edge crosses the pupil; the patient looks through the lens and past it at the same time and gets two images. `cat-10` prints it in those words — *\"the displaced lens edge is seen crossing the pupil, and it gives UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA.\"*\n\n**⚠️ The other physical sign of the same lens is worth having: PHACODONESIS**, a lens that wobbles with eye movement, which is the *\"irregular lens edge shimmering with movement\"* the bank uses in `opqb-t18-733`. *(The word phacodonesis is printed by no cached deck — __not taken from the course material__.)*\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A miotic pupil** | A small pupil narrows the aperture, which **reduces** aberration and if anything sharpens the image. ⚠️ It is also the wrong sign for trauma in general: `orb-13` records against `L22) Pupil and Visual pathway` that traumatic iritis and a ruptured globe give miosis, while orbital pressure gives mydriasis — but neither produces diplopia. |\n| **An ectropion uveac** [sic, for *ectropion uveae*] | Posterior iris pigment epithelium rolled forward onto the anterior iris surface at the pupil margin. It is a feature of **rubeosis iridis** and of congenital anomalies (`op-uvea`), it is a pigment finding, and it does not split an image. |\n| **A Vossius ring** | ⚠️ **The most tempting wrong answer, because it IS a blunt-trauma lens sign** — pigment stamped on the anterior capsule (`opqb-t18-733`). **But it is an imprint on the capsule surface, not a displacement of the lens.** It sits outside the axis, it is faint, and it causes no symptom at all. **A sign of the injury, not a cause of the diplopia.** |\n\n**⚠️ AND THE SAME BANK KEYS THIS DESCRIPTION DIFFERENTLY TWO QUESTIONS LATER.** `opqb-t18-747` prints an all-but-identical vignette and keys **C, the retina** — see that entry, where the disagreement is recorded in full. **Both are keyed as printed.**\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` “Cataract — types, maturity and clinical picture” — `cat-10` carries the displaced lens.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Double vision that persists when the fellow eye is covered is monocular, so the cause is inside the eye — here a subluxated lens whose equator crosses the pupil, which is one of the deck’s two uniocular-diplopia lesions.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q745), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-746', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'Trauma to the eye **cannot** cause:',
    options:['Vitreous hemorrhage.','Retinal breaks.','Macular edema.','Central retinal vein occlusion.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three of the four are on `L25) Trauma I`’s own blunt-trauma list. The fourth needs a mechanism trauma does not supply.**\n\n| Option | Where the module puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage** | `L25` slide 60, step 3 of the focused examination: ophthalmoscopy asks **\"?? Retinal Detachment · ?? Vitreous Hemorrhage\"** — the deck expects to find it. Blood reaches the vitreous from a torn retinal vessel, a choroidal rupture or a retinal tear. |\n| **Retinal breaks** | `L25` slides 37–38, **\"Blunt trauma: Retinal breaks ± Retinal detachment\"**, photographs labelled RETINAL TEAR and RETINAL DETACHMENT. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` names trauma as a cause of rhegmatogenous detachment, and `opqb-t13-569` and `opqb-t13-571` are both post-traumatic tear vignettes. |\n| **Macular edema** | `L25` slides 35–36 give **commotio retinae, \"Berlin’s EDEMA\"**, with **RETINAL EDEMA** printed on the photograph. The deck’s own word. |\n\n**⚠️ WHY A CENTRAL RETINAL VEIN OCCLUSION IS THE EXCEPTION, AND IT IS ABOUT WHO GETS ONE.** `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` and `op-ret-vasc` build CRVO out of **stagnant blood in a vessel already narrowed by disease**: the artery and the vein share an adventitial sheath at the lamina cribrosa, a **sclerotic artery compresses the vein**, flow slows and thromboses. The risk factors are **age, hypertension, diabetes, hyperviscosity and glaucoma** — a systemic and vascular list. **Trauma does not sclerose an artery and does not thicken the blood.** ⚠️ Note the contrast the module draws twice: a **branch** vein occlusion happens at an **arteriovenous crossing** where the artery presses on the vein (`opqb-t19-776`), and a **central** one at the lamina. Both are compressions by a diseased artery, not by an outside blow.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE ARTERIAL SIDE IS THE ONE PLACE TRAUMA DOES REACH THE RETINAL CIRCULATION.** `L25` slide 27 lists **CRAO** among the complications of hyphaema with uncontrolled high IOP — orbital or intraocular pressure can exceed arterial perfusion pressure and stop the flow. **There is no venous equivalent on any slide.** So the module’s position is precise: trauma can occlude the artery through pressure; it does not occlude the vein.\n\n**⚠️ Read this beside `opqb-t18-743`**, which uses the same CRVO/CRAO separation from the other direction — a cherry-red spot appears in every option except the vein occlusion, because a CRVO fundus is engorged rather than pale.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret-vasc` “Retinal vascular disorders” — the pathogenesis of CRVO is `vsc-4`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Blunt trauma produces vitreous haemorrhage, retinal breaks and macular oedema; it does not produce a central retinal vein occlusion, which needs a sclerotic artery compressing the vein at the lamina cribrosa.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q746), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-747', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient had a **blunt trauma to his left eye** followed by **double vision that disappears on covering the left eye only**. The search for the **etiology** of this condition would be in:',
    options:['The angle of the anterior chamber','The extraocular muscles.','The retina','The iris'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A DISAGREEMENT WITH THE PRINTED KEY, RECORDED AND NOT DISPUTED. The key is C and it is keyed C here. Every source in the module points somewhere else, and so does this bank two questions earlier.**\n\n**What the stem describes.** *Double vision that disappears on covering the left eye ONLY* means it does **not** disappear on covering the right — the left eye still sees double by itself. That is **monocular (uniocular) diplopia**, and `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.42**, Table (1-3), defines it exactly so: **\"double vision in one eye… REMAINS EVEN AFTER OCCLUSION of the other eye.\"**\n\n**What the module lists as its causes — and the retina is not among them.** The same table prints five:\n\n> **Lens subluxation · Iridodialysis · Incipient cataract · Astigmatism · Mucous strands in the tear film**\n\nAnd `L25) Trauma I` narrows it further for a blunt injury, labelling **exactly two** of its lesions **\"(UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"**: **iridodialysis** (slide 29) and **lens subluxation** (slide 34). **Of the four options offered, the deck’s own answer is D — the iris**, since an iris root torn from the ciliary body leaves a second aperture through which a second image reaches the retina.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE SAME BANK KEYED THE SAME DESCRIPTION TO THE LENS TWO QUESTIONS AGO.** `opqb-t18-745` prints an all-but-identical vignette — blunt trauma, left eye, diplopia surviving occlusion of the right — and keys **A, \"a pupil showing the lens equator\"**, the subluxated lens. **Two questions, one phenomenon, three different structures between them.** Both are keyed as printed and neither is folded, because folding either would hide the inconsistency.\n\n**Why the key might have been intended, in fairness to it.** Macular pathology — an **epiretinal membrane** distorting the photoreceptor mosaic — can produce monocular double vision or metamorphopsia. *(__Not taken from the course material__; no cached deck or book chapter names a retinal cause of monocular diplopia.)* But an epiretinal membrane is not a blunt-trauma lesion in this module, and `L25`’s trauma-and-retina slides (35–39) give commotio, breaks, detachment and choroidal rupture — none of which is listed as causing diplopia.\n\n**Why the other two:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **The angle of the anterior chamber** | **Angle recession** is a real blunt-trauma lesion and it does matter — it causes late secondary glaucoma. But the angle is not in the optical path and recession causes no diplopia. ⚠️ It is a near-miss for option D, because the angle is where the iris root sits. |\n| **The extraocular muscles** | ⚠️ **The answer that the stem is built to exclude, and the reason the question exists.** Muscle and nerve causes give **BINOCULAR** diplopia — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists III, IV and VI nerve palsy, myasthenia, muscle restriction or entrapment and thyroid ophthalmopathy on the *other* side of the table, all of which **disappear when either eye is covered**. The entrapped inferior rectus of a blow-out fracture (`opqb-t18-754`) is exactly this, and it is exactly what this stem rules out. |\n\n**How to hold this in an exam.** **Learn the rule, not the key.** Diplopia that survives occlusion of the fellow eye is caused inside the eye — lens, iris, cornea, tear film — and after blunt trauma it is a **subluxated lens or an iridodialysis** until proved otherwise.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat` “Cataract — types, maturity and clinical picture” — `cat-10` carries the displaced lens; the iridodialysis is `trm-7` and `opqb-t18-751`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Diplopia that persists when the fellow eye is covered is monocular and its causes are intraocular — the module lists lens subluxation, iridodialysis, incipient cataract, astigmatism and tear-film mucus, and names no retinal cause.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q747), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-748', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **child with a recent history of ocular trauma** complains of **blurry vision**. Ocular examination reveals **visual acuity of 6/9 in the right eye and 6/9 in the left eye**. **Ocular motility was normal. The examination of the fundus was normal.** The next step in management would be:',
    options:['Performing a fluorescein angiogram.','Ordering a field of vision.','Performing ocular ultrasound.','Performing a proper refraction.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Read the numbers before the story. The trauma in this stem is a distraction, and the examination has already excluded everything it could have caused.**\n\n| What the stem gives | What it rules out |\n|---|---|\n| **6/9 in BOTH eyes — equal, and only mildly down** | A unilateral injury does not produce a **symmetrical** reduction. Whatever is blurring this child is affecting both eyes equally, so it is not the blow. |\n| **Normal ocular motility** | No entrapment, no orbital fracture, no cranial nerve palsy — the `opqb-t18-754` group is excluded. |\n| **Normal fundus** | No commotio retinae, no retinal break or detachment, no choroidal rupture, no vitreous haemorrhage, no optic disc abnormality — `L25) Trauma I` slides 35–39 and slide 60’s ophthalmoscopy step are all clear. |\n\n**What is left is the one thing that has not been tested: the refraction.** `L25` slide 59 puts **visual acuity** in step 2 of the focused examination, but an acuity is only ever a *number until it is refracted* — an uncorrected refractive error and a pathological cause of blurring produce the same unaided reading. **6/9 in both eyes in a child is the acuity of an uncorrected low ametropia, most often astigmatism or mild hyperopia.** *(The typical error is __not taken from the course material__; the refraction step is `op-refract`’s and `op-intro-exam`’s.)*\n\n**⚠️ AND THE HISTORY EXPLAINS WHY IT SURFACED NOW.** A child with a mild uncorrected error usually has no complaint — he has never known better. **The trauma brought him to an ophthalmologist for the first time**, and the first person to measure his acuity found it was not 6/6. The injury did not cause the blur; it caused the visit.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | What it would be for, and why not here |\n|---|---|\n| **Fluorescein angiogram** | Maps **leakage and perfusion** — `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` uses it for focal leakage, diffuse leakage and capillary non-perfusion. ⚠️ **It is an investigation of an abnormal fundus, and this fundus is normal.** It is also an intravenous dye study in a child, which is not a screening test. |\n| **Ordering a field of vision** | Perimetry is for a **suspected neurological or glaucomatous** defect and needs a cooperative patient. Nothing in the stem suggests a field defect, and a young child cannot reliably perform automated perimetry. |\n| **Performing ocular ultrasound** | ⚠️ **The right tool for the wrong eye, and the module says so repeatedly.** B-scan is for **opaque media** — `opqb-t13-550` keys it for a diabetic with rapid visual deterioration, `opqb-t9-376` for a diabetic whose fundus cannot be seen through his cataracts, and `L25` slide 52 for an IOFB behind blood. **This child’s fundus was seen and was normal, so there is nothing to scan through.** |\n\n**⚠️ The general lesson, which is worth more than the mark.** In a post-trauma clinic the reflex is to reach for imaging. **The cheapest test is often the diagnostic one**, and an acuity that is not 6/6 has not been explained until someone has put a lens in front of it.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-refract` “Errors of refraction — the optics, myopia and hyperopia” — the refraction itself.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Equal, mildly reduced acuity with normal motility and a normal fundus after trauma is an uncorrected refractive error found incidentally — refract before imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q748), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-749', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A patient with a **recent history of a motor car accident** complains of **blurry vision together with a tendency to vomiting**. Examination of the **visual acuity was 6/6 in each eye**. The next step in management would be:',
    options:['Performing a CT scan.','Examining the ocular motility.','Performing the cover test.','Plotting of the visual field.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**This is the mirror of `opqb-t18-748` — the same sentence frame, and every variable changed to point away from the eye.**\n\n| What the stem gives | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **6/6 in EACH eye** | **The eyes are normal.** Not mildly reduced, not asymmetrical — full acuity in both. Nothing ophthalmic is causing this. |\n| **A motor car accident** | A **high-energy head injury**, not the fist or ball of the blunt-eye vignettes. `L25) Trauma I` slide 56 puts the **mechanism of injury** first in the focused history precisely because the mechanism predicts what else was hit. |\n| **VOMITING** | ⚠️ **The word that moves this out of ophthalmology altogether.** Vomiting after a head injury, with blurred vision and normal eyes, is **raised intracranial pressure** — an intracranial haemorrhage until proved otherwise. |\n\n**CT of the head is the investigation, and it is urgent.** It is fast, available in casualty, and it is the modality that shows **acute blood, fracture and midline shift**. *(The intracranial indication is __not taken from the course material__ — no cached ophthalmology deck teaches head-injury triage. What the module supplies is `L25` slide 56’s insistence on mechanism, and `L10) orbit`’s use of CT for orbital trauma.)*\n\n**⚠️ AND THE OPHTHALMIC SIGN TO LOOK FOR IS THE DISC, WHICH IS ALSO NOT ON THE OPTION LIST.** `L21) Optic Nerve` gives papilloedema the single aetiology **\"↑ ICP\"**, and `onv-4` and `onv-5` describe it. Blurred vision with normal acuity and vomiting is the classic early combination, and the fundus is where an ophthalmologist contributes — **but the fundus takes minutes and the bleed does not wait, so the scan comes first.**\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Examining the ocular motility** | ⚠️ **The best distractor**, because motility genuinely is the next step in a different road-traffic patient — the one with an **orbital blow-out fracture** and diplopia (`opqb-t18-754`, `opmcq-c3-12`). **But this patient does not complain of double vision**, and a motility examination that takes five minutes cannot be allowed to delay a scan in someone who is vomiting. |\n| **Performing the cover test** | A test for **strabismus and heterophoria** (`op-squint`). It answers a question about ocular alignment that nothing in the stem has raised. |\n| **Plotting of the visual field** | Perimetry takes fifteen to twenty minutes per eye and needs a settled, cooperative patient. ⚠️ **Asking someone who is about to vomit to fixate a target for twenty minutes is not a plan**, and a field defect from an intracranial lesion would be found on the CT far sooner. |\n\n**⚠️ The pair to carry away.** `opqb-t18-748`: **blurred vision, acuity DOWN, everything else normal → refract.** `opqb-t18-749`: **blurred vision, acuity NORMAL, systemic symptom → look outside the eye.** The bank has printed them adjacently on purpose.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-neuro-optic` “The optic nerve” — papilloedema from raised intracranial pressure is `onv-4`.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Blurred vision with 6/6 acuity and vomiting after a road traffic accident is raised intracranial pressure, not an ocular injury — the next step is a CT of the head, not an eye test.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q749), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-750', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'A **full thickness wound of the eyewall caused by a BLUNT object** is:',
    options:['Rupture.','Penetrating injury.','Perforating Injury.','Lamellar laceration.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slides 43–45 draw the distinction in three lines, and the whole of it turns on the object:**\n\n> **RUPTURE GLOBE (BLUNT OBJECT)**\n> Partial or full thickness **LACERATION (SHARP OBJECT)**\n> **OPEN-GLOBE INJURY:** (rupture globe **or** full thickness laceration) → **REPAIRED WITH SUTURES**\n\n**⚠️ THE MECHANISM IS INSIDE-OUT, WHICH IS WHY IT NEEDS A DIFFERENT WORD.** A blunt object cannot cut. What it does is compress the globe along one axis, which raises the intraocular pressure abruptly and makes the eye burst **from within**, at its weakest points — the limbus, behind the rectus insertions where the sclera is thinnest, and around any previous surgical wound. **A laceration is made from outside in by an edge; a rupture is made from inside out by pressure.** *(The sites of weakness are __not taken from the course material__; the blunt/sharp split is the deck’s.)*\n\n**⚠️ AND BOTH ROUTES END IN THE SAME PLACE.** `L25` slide 45 puts rupture and full-thickness laceration together under **OPEN-GLOBE INJURY**, and once the globe is open the protocol is identical whatever made the hole: **shield, call, do not patch, do not check the pressure, no drops, nil by mouth** (slide 51, and `opqb-t18-736` and `opqb-t18-737`).\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | What it actually names |\n|---|---|\n| **Penetrating injury** | A **sharp** object making **one** full-thickness wound — it goes in and stays in. ⚠️ The word the stem excludes is *blunt*. |\n| **Perforating Injury** | A **sharp** object making **TWO** full-thickness wounds — an entry and an exit. **The distinction between penetrating and perforating is the number of holes**, and it is the pair students most often invert. *(The one-wound/two-wound definition is __not taken from the course material__; `L25` says only \"partial or full thickness laceration (sharp object)\".)* |\n| **Lamellar laceration** | ⚠️ **The right half of the deck’s own sentence, and the wrong depth.** `L25` slide 44 offers **\"PARTIAL or full thickness laceration\"**; a lamellar laceration is the partial-thickness one — a groove in the sclera or cornea that does not reach the inside. **The stem says FULL thickness**, so this is excluded twice over: wrong depth and wrong object. |\n\n**⚠️ The clinical value of the word.** Calling it a *rupture* tells the surgeon to expect a wound that is **larger, more posterior and more ragged** than the visible injury suggests, often hidden under intact conjunctiva — which is why `L25` slides 49–50 list a **bullous subconjunctival haemorrhage** among the signs of a suspected open globe.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A full-thickness eyewall wound made by a blunt object is a rupture — the globe bursts from within under sudden pressure; lacerations, penetrating and perforating injuries are all made by sharp objects from outside.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q750), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-751', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**Iridodialysis**:',
    options:['Separation of the iris root from ciliary body.','Patient complains from binocular diplopia.','Pupil is round and regular.','Always associated with retinal dialysis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Option A is the definition, and the module names this lesion five times without ever defining it.**\n\n⚠️ **MEASURED:** `iridodialysis` returns hits in `L25) Trauma I` slide 29, `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 8, `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination`, `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.1 and ch.10 — **and every one of them is a bare name or a figure caption.** `L13,14` slide 8 is literally the words *\"Iris defects: Coloboma, Iridodialysis, Iridectomy & Aniridia\"* and the cache records **\"These are headings only. Nothing on this slide is defined or explained.\"** Book chapter 10 says the same of its own page: **\"Six named iris conditions are LISTED and PICTURED but NOT DEFINED anywhere in the chapter. Any teaching on them must come from elsewhere.\"** *(The definition below is __not taken from the course material__; `trm-7` records the same gap.)*\n\n**What it is.** A blunt blow drives the equator of the globe outward and shears the **iris root**, its thinnest part, away from its attachment to the **ciliary body**. The result is a **D-shaped pupil** with a crescentic gap at the periphery through which the red reflex or the lens equator can be seen — a **second aperture into the eye**.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE SAME TEAR IS WHAT BLEEDS.** `opqb-t18-729`’s printed box gives the source of a traumatic hyphaema as **\"the iris root\"** — so an iridodialysis and a hyphaema are the same injury seen at two moments, one as blood and one as a hole.\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Patient complains from binocular diplopia** [sic, for *complains OF*] | ⚠️ **The word is wrong and it is the whole point.** `L25` slide 29 prints the label in capitals: **\"Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"**. It is one of only **two** lesions the deck labels that way (the other is lens subluxation, slide 34), and `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.42** lists iridodialysis among the causes of **monocular** diplopia. **The two apertures are both in the same eye**, so the double image survives covering the fellow eye. Binocular diplopia is a muscle or nerve problem, which this is not. |\n| **Pupil is round and regular** | The opposite of the sign. The pupil is **distorted** — the classic **D shape** — and the distortion is often the only visible evidence of the tear. |\n| **Always associated with retinal dialysis** | ⚠️ **A word-play distractor, and it is the strongest wrong answer.** *Retinal dialysis* is a real and genuinely traumatic lesion — a **circumferential disinsertion of the retina at the ora serrata**, the break most characteristic of blunt injury (`rd-9`) — and `opqb-t18-735` offers it as a distractor too. **But *dialysis* here just means \"a separation\", and the two separations are at opposite ends of the eye.** One blow can cause both; neither requires the other, and \"always\" makes the option false on its own. |\n\n**⚠️ Why it matters beyond the pupil.** A second aperture means **glare, photophobia and monocular diplopia**; a large one is repaired surgically. And because the tear sits at the angle, an iridodialysis is a marker for **angle recession** and therefore for late secondary glaucoma. *(Both consequences are __not taken from the course material__.)*\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-uvea` “Uvea” — `uv-12` is the iris-disease page that lists this condition without defining it.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Iridodialysis is separation of the iris root from the ciliary body after a blunt blow — it distorts the pupil, gives uniocular diplopia through a second aperture, and is the source of a traumatic hyphaema.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.118 (Q751), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-752', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat',
    stem:'**Lens subluxation** presents with:',
    options:['Monocular diplopia','High degrees of astigmatism.','Impaired accommodation.','All of the above'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three are consequences of one event — the zonules tearing partially, so the lens is still in the pupil but no longer held properly.**\n\n| Feature | Why a partly detached lens produces it |\n|---|---|\n| **Monocular diplopia** | ✅ **Printed by the module twice.** `L25) Trauma I` slide 34: **\"Subluxation — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"**, one of only two lesions the deck labels that way. `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.42**, Table (1-3), lists **lens subluxation** first among the causes of monocular diplopia. The mechanism is `cat-10`’s: **the displaced lens edge is seen crossing the pupil**, so the patient looks through the lens and past it at once. |\n| **High degrees of astigmatism** | ✅ **Also printed, in the same book table.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists **astigmatism** among the monocular diplopia causes; a tilted lens is exactly that — its two principal meridians no longer share a focus, so the refraction becomes **lenticular astigmatism**, often of a degree no spectacle lens corrects comfortably. *(The word \"lenticular\" and the tilt mechanism are __not taken from the course material__.)* |\n| **Impaired accommodation** | ⚠️ **The one the decks do not print, and it follows from the anatomy they do.** Accommodation works because the ciliary muscle contracts, the **zonules slacken** and the lens rounds up. **Torn zonules cannot transmit that.** The zonular apparatus is the whole mechanism, so a lens hanging on partial zonules cannot change shape reliably. *(__Not taken from the course material__ — neither trauma deck nor either cataract deck states it.)* |\n\n**⚠️ WHICH MAKES \"ALL OF THE ABOVE\" THE ONLY ANSWER THAT DOES NOT REQUIRE DISCARDING A TRUE STATEMENT.** Two of the three are printed in this module and the third follows necessarily from the anatomy of accommodation.\n\n**⚠️ Subluxation against dislocation, because the bank uses both words.** `cat-10`: **subluxation is PARTIAL zonular rupture — the lens is still in the pupillary area**; **complete displacement is DISLOCATION**, and the lens falls into the vitreous or comes forward into the anterior chamber. **A dislocated lens gives aphakia in that eye, not diplopia** — there is no lens edge left in the axis to split the image. The three findings above all belong to the *partial* stage.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE TWO SYNDROMIC DIRECTIONS ARE THE MODULE’S FAVOURITE PAIR** (`L18) Cataract II`, and `opmcq-c9-23`):\n\n| Condition | Direction | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Marfan syndrome** | **supero-temporal** | the **inferior** zonules stay intact, so the lens is held up |\n| **Homocystinuria** | **infero-nasal** | **all** the zonules are weak, so the lens simply falls |\n\n**Trauma is the third cause and it has no preferred direction** — the lens goes wherever the zonules gave way.\n\n**⚠️ The clinical signs to look for:** the **edge of the lens crossing the pupil**, **phacodonesis** (a wobbling lens — the *\"irregular lens edge shimmering with movement\"* of `opqb-t18-733`) and **iridodonesis**, a tremulous iris that has lost its support, which is what `opmcq-c9-17` asks about.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — `trm-7` carries subluxation as one of the two uniocular-diplopia lesions of blunt injury.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A partly subluxated lens gives monocular diplopia from its edge crossing the pupil, high lenticular astigmatism from its tilt, and impaired accommodation because torn zonules cannot transmit ciliary contraction.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.119 (Q752), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-753', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'All these measures are used in **treating hyphaema except**:',
    options:['Bed rest in supine position.','Topical steroids.','Topical beta blocker.','Oral aminocaproic acid'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**`L25) Trauma I` slide 28 prints the regimen, and the posture in it is not supine:**\n\n> Hospitalization: NOT EVERY CASE (children, sickle cell disease, uncontrolled high IOP, coagulation or bleeding disorders) · **BED REST (45 DEGREE)** · Cycloplegics (atropine) · **Topical steroid** · **Antiglaucoma if increased IOP** · Surgical evacuation: persistent elevated IOP · total hyphaema > 5 days\n\n**⚠️ THE ANGLE IS THE TREATMENT, AND LYING FLAT UNDOES IT.** Sitting the patient up at 45 degrees lets gravity keep the blood layered **inferiorly**, below the pupil, where it clears through the trabecular meshwork without obstructing the visual axis. **Supine, the blood disperses across the whole anterior chamber**: it coats the corneal endothelium, blocks the axis, blocks more of the angle and — with the pressure up — is driven into the stroma as **blood staining of the cornea** (`L25` slide 27, and `opqb-t18-742`). ⚠️ **This is the exact complement of `opqb-t18-730`**, which keys the same posture as the correct initial management. One question asks for it, one asks for its inversion.\n\n**Why the other three ARE used:**\n\n| Option | Where it sits in the regimen |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical steroids** | Printed on the slide by name. The blood and the torn iris root provoke an inflammatory reaction; steroid quietens it and reduces the risk of **posterior synechiae** forming while the pupil is dilated on atropine. |\n| **Topical beta blocker** | The slide’s **\"Antiglaucoma if increased IOP\"**. A topical beta-blocker reduces aqueous **production**, which is the right lever when the outflow pathway is clogged with red cells. ⚠️ Note what is *not* used: a **miotic** such as pilocarpine, which would pull on the bleeding iris root (`opqb-t18-730`), and, in a sickle-cell patient, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, which acidify the aqueous and worsen sickling. *(The pilocarpine and sickling cautions are __not taken from the course material__.)* |\n| **Oral aminocaproic acid** | ⚠️ **ABSENT FROM THE COURSE MATERIAL, AND MEASURED AS ABSENT.** The bank prints it **\"aminocarpoic\"**; both `aminocaproic` AND `aminocarpoic` return **zero hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 cached book chapters**. It is nonetheless a real and long-established treatment: an **antifibrinolytic** that stabilises the clot over the torn vessel and so reduces the rate of **re-bleeding** — which `L25` slide 27 names as the leading complication, **\"common in the FIRST 5 DAYS\"**. It fits the deck’s own stated danger exactly, and it is therefore not the exception. *(__Not taken from the course material__.)* |\n\n**⚠️ The unifying idea behind the whole regimen.** Every measure is aimed at the same two enemies from `L25` slide 27 — **the re-bleed** and **the pressure**. Rest, an upright head, atropine and an antifibrinolytic protect the clot; the beta-blocker and, failing that, surgical evacuation protect the nerve and the cornea.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hyphaema is rested at 45 degrees, not supine, so the blood layers below the visual axis; topical steroid, a topical beta-blocker for the pressure and an antifibrinolytic against re-bleeding are all part of the regimen.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.119 (Q753), key p.120' },

  { id:'opqb-t18-754', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-orbit-mass',
    stem:'The **most common defective eye movement** in **blow-out fracture of the orbit** is:',
    options:['Elevation.','Depression.','Adduction.','Abduction.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The floor breaks, and the muscles that sit on the floor are the ones that get caught.**\n\n`L25) Trauma I` slide 21 names them: restrictive ocular motility from **\"MECHANICAL ENTRAPMENT of the INFERIOR OBLIQUE or INFERIOR RECTUS muscles within the fracture, or adjacent connective tissue and fat.\"** `ophthalmology.pdf` **p.59** prints the same pair in the same words.\n\n**⚠️ THE DIRECTION OF THE DEFECT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE INSTINCTIVE ANSWER, AND THIS IS THE TRAP.** An entrapped **inferior rectus** is a **depressor** — so the instinct is to say depression fails. **It does not.** A tethered muscle is not a weak muscle: it is a rope tied to the floor of the orbit. The eye can still be pulled **towards** it, but it cannot be pulled **away** from it. **So the entrapped inferior rectus blocks ELEVATION**, and the patient’s diplopia is worst looking **up**.\n\n> **A tethered muscle limits movement in the direction OPPOSITE to its own action. A paralysed muscle limits movement in the direction OF its action.** That single sentence separates a restrictive from a neurogenic motility defect, and `orb-14` flags it as the same trap as the fibrosed inferior rectus of thyroid eye disease in `orb-6`.\n\n**⚠️ AND THE DECK PRINTS SOMETHING SLIGHTLY DIFFERENT, WHICH IS WORTH HOLDING BESIDE THE KEY.** `L25` slide 21 says diplopia occurs **\"in BOTH UPGAZE AND DOWNGAZE\"** — because the floor fragment can tether tissue in a way that limits the eye at both ends of its vertical travel, and because early haemorrhage and oedema restrict movement generally (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.59 gives that as the first of two causes of the diplopia, adding that it **\"usually improves as the haemorrhage and oedema resolve\"**). **The bank asks which is MOST common, and the answer is the one entrapment produces mechanically: elevation.**\n\n**Why the other three:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Depression** | The direction the entrapped muscle can still pull the eye. It fails only if the *superior* structures are tethered, which a floor fracture does not do. |\n| **Adduction** | A **medial rectus** action. It would be limited by a **MEDIAL WALL** fracture through the **lamina papyracea** — which `ophthalmology.pdf` p.58 says happens **\"sometimes\"**, second to the floor. ⚠️ **Second is not most common**, and `trm-7` records medial-wall entrapment as a gap neither trauma deck teaches. |\n| **Abduction** | A **lateral rectus** action. The lateral wall is the thickest wall of the orbit and does not blow out. ⚠️ Limited abduction after head trauma is far more likely to be a **sixth nerve palsy** — `opqb-t14-625` gives trauma as a cause — which is a *neurogenic* defect, not a restrictive one, and is distinguished by the **forced duction test**. ⚠️ MEASURED: the forced duction test is named by **no cached deck** — `L25` slide 59 gestures at it with **\"(ENTRAPPED MUSCLES?)\"** under EOMs and `L10) orbit` slide 10’s sentence about it is **cut off by the slide edge**. *(__Not taken from the course material__.)* |\n\n**⚠️ The rest of the syndrome, for recognition:** ecchymosis and oedema, **subcutaneous emphysema** as the earliest sign (`opqb-t3-133`), **infraorbital nerve anaesthesia** over the lower lid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip and upper teeth (`opmcq-c3-1`), **late enophthalmos**, and on **coronal CT** the **tear drop sign** of tissue herniating into the maxillary sinus. And one instruction: **do not blow the nose.**\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-trauma` “Trauma” — the blunt injury itself; `orb-14` carries the full blow-out account.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A blow-out fracture entraps the inferior rectus or inferior oblique in the orbital floor, and a tethered muscle limits movement away from itself — so elevation is the movement that fails.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.119 (Q754), key p.120' }
